The '''M23 campaign''' is an ongoing series of [[Offensive (military)|military offensives]] launched by the [[March 23 Movement]] (M23), a Rwandan-backed rebel paramilitary group in the [[Democratic Republic of the Congo]], since March 2022. In November 2021, M23 first launched attacks against the [[Armed Forces of the Democratic Republic of the Congo|Congolese military]] (FARDC) and [[MONUSCO]], seizing military positions in Ndiza, Cyanzu, and Runyoni in [[North Kivu|North Kivu Province]].<ref name="auto1">{{cite web |first= |date=6 August 2024 |title=The Resurgence of the M23: Regional Rivalries, Donor Policy, and a Stalled Peace Process |url=https://cic.nyu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/The-Resurgence-of-the-M23-EN.pdf |access-date=1 February 2025 |website=Ebuteli |publisher=Congo Research Group (CRG) |pages=17–20 |ref={{harvid|CRG|2024}}}}</ref> This coincided with the deployment of [[Uganda People's Defence Force]] (UPDF) to the region to combat the [[Allied Democratic Forces]] (ADF), a Ugandan rebel group operating in the Congo's North Kivu and [[Ituri Province|Ituri]] provinces.<ref name=":622">{{cite web |last= |first= |date=11 July 2022 |title=Le Rwanda et la RDC risquent la guerre avec l'émergence de la nouvelle rébellion du M23: Une explication |trans-title=Rwanda and DRC risk war with the emergence of the new M23 rebellion: An explanation |url=https://africacenter.org/fr/spotlight/le-rwanda-et-la-rdc-risquent-la-guerre-avec-lemergence-de-la-nouvelle-rebellion-du-m23-une-explication/ |access-date=20 February 2025 |website=[[Africa Center for Strategic Studies]] |language=fr-FR}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=3 December 2021 |title=DRC-Uganda: Civilians must be protected during joint military operations |url=https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2021/12/drc-uganda-civilians-must-be-protected-during-joint-military-operations/ |access-date=20 February 2025 |website=[[Amnesty International]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=14 June 2022 |title=Uganda's Operation Shujaa in the Democratic Republic of Congo: Fighting the ADF or Securing Economic Interests? |url=https://cic.nyu.edu/wp-content/uploads/2022/10/report-crg-ebuteli-uganda-operation-shujaa-drc-adf-securing-economic-interests-1.pdf |access-date=20 February 2025 |website=[[Center on International Cooperation at New York University|Center on International Cooperation]] |page=4}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=29 November 2021 |title=DR Congo 'authorises' Ugandan troops to hunt rebel group on its soil |url=https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20211129-dr-congo-authorises-ugandan-troops-to-hunt-rebel-group-on-its-soil |access-date=20 February 2025 |publisher=[[France 24]]}}</ref>

The conflict escalated between March and June 2022, as M23 overran key areas in [[Rutshuru Territory]], including the strategic border town of [[Bunagana, Democratic Republic of the Congo|Bunagana]], forcing Congolese soldiers to flee into Uganda.<ref name="schlindwein"/><ref name="auto1"/><ref name=":622"/><ref name="Schwikowski">{{cite news |author=Schwikowski |first=Martina |date=8 April 2022 |title=M23 rebels resurface in DR Congo |url=https://www.dw.com/en/m23-rebels-resurface-in-dr-congo/a-61383104 |access-date=13 June 2022 |work=[[DW News]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220609161156/https://www.dw.com/en/m23-rebels-resurface-in-dr-congo/a-61383104 |archive-date=9 June 2022|url-status=live}}</ref> Uganda alleged that [[Rwanda]] orchestrated the offensive to undermine UPDF operations against the ADF, while Rwanda counterclaimed that Uganda was leveraging M23 elements to threaten its national security.<ref name=":622"/> The DRC accused Rwanda of provisioning armaments and reorganizing the insurgency, a claim substantiated by a [[United Nations Security Council]] (UNSC) [[United Nations Group of Experts on Geographical Names|Group of Experts]] report.<ref name=":622"/> Rwanda and M23, in turn, accused the DRC of collaborating with the [[Democratic Forces for the Liberation of Rwanda]] (FDLR) and claimed their campaign aimed to protect [[Banyamulenge]] from FDLR aggression.<ref>{{Cite web |date=30 November 2022 |title=Regional Powers Should Drive Diplomacy in DR Congo as M23 Surrounds Goma |url=https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/great-lakes/democratic-republic-congo/regional-powers-should-drive-diplomacy-dr-congo-m23 |access-date=20 February 2025 |website=[[International Crisis Group]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=25 May 2022 |title=Apaiser les Tensions dans l'Est de la RD Congo et les Grands Lacs |trans-title=Easing Tensions in Eastern DR Congo and the Great Lakes |url=https://www.crisisgroup.org/fr/africa/great-lakes/democratic-republic-congo/b181-easing-turmoil-eastern-dr-congo-and-great-lakes |access-date=20 February 2025 |website=[[International Crisis Group]]}}</ref> A UNSC report noted that Rwandan military incursions into Congolese territory had begun prior to alleged FARDC-FDLR cooperation, with analysts posited that M23's resurgence was primarily driven by economic and commercial interests rather than ethno-political or security concerns.<ref name=":622"/><ref name="StearnsAug2022">{{cite web |last=Stearns |first=Jason |date=17 August 2022 |title=Faut-il parler des FDLR à chaque fois qu'on évoque le M23? |trans-title=Should we talk about the FDLR every time we talk about the M23? |url=https://www.congoresearchgroup.org/fr/2022/08/17/faut-il-parler-des-fdlr-a-chaque-fois-quon-evoque-le-m23/ |access-date=17 February 2025 |website=Congo Research Group (Groupe d'étude sur le Congo) |language=fr}}</ref>

The conflict drew regional involvement, leading the [[East African Community]] (EAC) to deploy the [[East African Community Regional Force]] (EACRF) to stabilize the situation.<ref name=":922">{{harvnb|CRG|2024|pages=21–22}}</ref> On 26 January 2023, M23 captured [[Kitchanga]].<ref name=":92322">{{harvnb|CRG|2024|pages=25–26}}</ref> Exasperated by the perceived inaction of the EACRF, the Congolese government sought military assistance from the [[Southern African Development Community]] (SADC) and established a reserve corps, which encouraged the formation of militias under the [[Wazalendo]] movement near M23-controlled areas.<ref name=":923222">{{harvnb|CRG|2024|pages=27–30}}</ref> In June 2023, [[Human Rights Watch]] documented widespread human rights abuses by M23, including [[Extrajudicial killing|extrajudicial executions]], [[sexual violence]], and other [[war crime]]s, with allegations of Rwandan complicity.<ref name=":63">{{Cite web |date=13 June 2023 |title=DR Congo: Killings, Rapes by Rwanda-Backed M23 Rebels |url=https://www.hrw.org/news/2023/06/13/dr-congo-killings-rapes-rwanda-backed-m23-rebels |access-date=31 January 2024 |website=[[Human Rights Watch]] |language=en}}</ref> The UNSC subsequently called for sanctions against M23 leaders and implicated high-ranking Rwandan officials in the violence.<ref name=":63" /> By March 2024, M23 had launched further offensives, including a northern push into Rutshuru Territory, capturing [[Rwindi]] and the Vitshumbi fishery along [[Lake Edward]].<ref name=":9232222">{{harvnb|CRG|2024|pages=31–32}}</ref> An April UNSC-commissioned report estimated that between 3,000 and 4,000 [[Rwandan Defence Force]] (RDF) troops were present in eastern DRC, surpassing the estimated 3,000 M23 combatants.<ref name=":9232222" /><ref name="Fr24Jul9">{{Cite web |date=8 July 2024 |title=Rwandan soldiers fighting with M23 rebels in DR Congo, says UN report |url=https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20240708-rwandan-soldiers-fighting-with-m23-rebels-in-dr-congo-says-un-report |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240709220618/https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20240708-rwandan-soldiers-fighting-with-m23-rebels-in-dr-congo-says-un-report |archive-date=9 July 2024 |access-date=10 July 2024 |website=[[France 24]] |language=en |publication-place=Paris, France}}</ref> In June 2024, M23 and RDF forces seized [[Kanyabayonga]] and Kirumba and entering [[Lubero Territory]] for the first time.<ref name=":9232222" /> Diplomatic efforts, led by Angolan President [[João Lourenço]], faltered after President [[Paul Kagame]] failed to attend a tripartite summit in [[Luanda]] on 15 December, which was meant to address the FDLR issue alongside President [[Félix Tshisekedi]] and President Lourenço.<ref name="Fabricius2024">{{Cite web |last=Fabricius |first=Peter |date=13 December 2024 |title=RDC-Rwanda: un accord de paix fragile |trans-title=DRC-Rwanda: a fragile peace agreement |url=https://issafrica.org/fr/iss-today/rdc-rwanda-un-accord-de-paix-fragile |access-date=17 February 2025 |website=[[Institute for Security Studies]] |language=en}}</ref><ref name="Bendhaou">{{Cite web |last=Bendhaou |first=Fatma |date=15 December 2024 |title=RDC-Rwanda-Angola: Le sommet tripartite entre les présidents Tshisekedi, Kagame et Lourenço annulé |trans-title=DRC-Rwanda-Angola: Tripartite summit between Presidents Tshisekedi, Kagame and Lourenço cancelled |url=https://www.aa.com.tr/fr/afrique/rdc-rwanda-angola-le-sommet-tripartite-entre-les-pr%C3%A9sidents-tshisekedi-kagame-et-lourenco-annul%C3%A9/3425295 |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=Aa.com.tr |language=fr}}</ref><ref name=":210">{{Cite web |last=Botela |first=Cédric |date=15 December 2024 |title=Crise à l'Est: Annulation du sommet tripartite RDC-Rwanda-Angola |trans-title=Crisis in the East: Cancellation of the tripartite summit DRC-Rwanda-Angola |url=https://www.congoquotidien.com/2024/12/15/actualite-rdc-crise-est-annulation/ |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=Congoquotidien.com |language=fr-FR}}</ref><ref name=":310">{{Cite web |date=15 December 2024 |title=Tripartite annulée à Luanda: Kagame absent, sommet réduit à un bilatéral Tshisekedi-Lourenço |trans-title=Tripartite cancelled in Luanda: Kagame absent, summit reduced to a bilateral Tshisekedi-Lourenço |url=https://actualite.cd/2024/12/15/tripartite-annulee-luanda-kagame-absent-sommet-reduit-un-bilateral-tshisekedi-lourenco |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref name=":410">{{Cite web |date=15 December 2024 |title=La tripartite RDC-Rwanda-Angola annulée |trans-title=The DRC-Rwanda-Angola tripartite cancelled |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2024/12/15/actualite/politique/la-tripartite-rdc-rwanda-angola-annulee |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Radio Okapi]] |language=fr}}</ref> Rwanda's absence fueled suspicions that its involvement in eastern DRC was driven primarily by economic interests, particularly access to [[Kivu]]'s [[Mining industry of the Democratic Republic of the Congo|mineral resources]], rather than security concerns.<ref name=":923">{{Cite web |date=16 February 2025 |title=Invasion rwandaise: le prétexte des FDLR en RDC détruit à l'UA |trans-title=Rwandan invasion: FDLR pretext in DRC destroyed at AU |url=https://www.mediacongo.net/article-actualite-147455_invasion_rwandaise_le_pretexte_des_fdlr_en_rdc_detruit_a_l_ua.html |access-date=17 February 2025 |website=Mediacongo.net |language=fr}}</ref><ref name="Lagarde2025">{{Cite web |last=De lagarde |first=Antoine |date=30 January 2025 |title="Quand l"Occident intervient au Congo, il est accusé de néocolonialisme et quand il n'intervient pas, il est accusé de s'en désintéresser" |trans-title="When the West intervenes in the Congo, it is accused of neocolonialism and when it does not intervene, it is accused of not being interested". |url=https://www.lefigaro.fr/vox/monde/quand-l-occident-intervient-au-congo-il-est-accuse-de-neocolonialisme-et-quand-il-n-intervient-pas-il-est-accuse-de-s-en-desinteresser-20250130 |access-date=17 February 2025 |website=[[Le Figaro]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Berthemet |first=Tanguy |date=29 January 2025 |title=RDC: le Kivu, une région riche en minerais, théâtre d'une guerre de trente ans |trans-title=DRC: Kivu, a region rich in minerals, scene of a thirty-year war |url=https://www.lefigaro.fr/international/regime-vermoulu-acces-aux-ressources-le-kivu-theatre-d-une-guerre-de-trente-ans-20250129 |access-date=21 February 2025 |website=[[Le Figaro]] |language=fr}}</ref>

Beginning in January 2025, M23 began making major advances towards [[Goma]] and [[Bukavu]], the provincial capitals of [[Nord-Kivu|North Kivu]] and [[Sud-Kivu|South Kivu]], with alleged Rwandan backing, intensifying [[Democratic Republic of the Congo–Rwanda conflict|growing tensions]] between the two nations. By 30 January, M23 had [[2025 Goma offensive|captured all of Goma]] and began an advance towards Bukavu, [[2025 Bukavu offensive|capturing the town]] by 16 February. Following the capture of Goma, M23 announced their intentions to march on [[Kinshasa]].<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Asadu |first1=Chinedu |last2=Kabumba |first2=Justin |date=30 January 2025 |title=Rwanda-backed rebels in eastern Congo say they plan to take their fight to the capital |url=https://apnews.com/article/congo-m23-goma-rwanda-tshisekedi-south-africa-ca81f34f52ea31b85dd845b049a0bb96 |access-date=1 February 2025 |website=[[Associated Press|AP News]] |language=en}}</ref> Despite the DRC and M23 endorsing a Declaration of Principles on 19 July in [[Doha]], which pledged peaceful dialogue, recognition of Congolese sovereignty, and respect for [[international law]], M23 persisted in carrying out its campaign.<ref name=":662">{{Cite web |last=Ligodi |first=Patient |date=19 July 2025 |title=Ce que contient la déclaration de principe signée à Doha par la RDC et l'AFC/M23 |trans-title=What is contained in the declaration of principle signed in Doha by the DRC and the AFC/M23 |url=https://www.rfi.fr/fr/afrique/20250719-ce-que-contient-la-d%C3%A9claration-de-principe-sign%C3%A9e-%C3%A0-doha-par-la-rdc-et-l-afc-m23 |access-date=19 July 2025 |website=[[Radio France Internationale]] |language=fr |publication-place=Paris, France}}</ref><ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=19 July 2025 |title=À Doha, le Gouvernement congolais et le M23/AFC signent une déclaration de principes pour mettre fin aux combats |trans-title=In Doha, the Congolese government and the M23/AFC sign a declaration of principles to end the fighting |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2025/07/19/actualite/securite/doha-le-gouvernement-congolais-et-le-m23afc-signent-une-declaration-de |access-date=19 July 2025 |website=[[Radio Okapi]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref name=":76">{{Cite web |last=Rigaud |first=Christophe |date=29 July 2025 |title=RDC: les accords de Washington et Doha au milieu du gué |trans-title=DRC: Washington and Doha agreements at a crossroads |url=https://afrikarabia.com/wordpress/rdc-les-accords-de-washington-et-doha-au-milieu-du-gue/ |access-date=14 September 2025 |website=Afrikarabia.com |language=fr-FR}}</ref>

== Background ==
{{further|M23 rebellion (2012–2013)|Kivu conflict}}
[[File:M23 in Goma.PNG|left|thumb|[[March 23 Movement|M23]] rebels in [[Goma]], November 2012]]

The [[March 23 Movement]] waged a [[M23 rebellion (2012–2013)|rebellion]] in the northeastern [[Democratic Republic of the Congo]] (DRC) from 2012 to 2013. M23 was formed by deserters of the [[National Congress for the Defence of the People]] (CNDP), who had integrated into the [[Armed Forces of the Democratic Republic of the Congo]] (FARDC) following a 2009 peace agreement.<ref name="BBC 30 March" /><ref name="HRW" /><ref name="Schwikowski"/> The agreement, which was negotiated after the arrest of CNDP leader General [[Laurent Nkunda]], included provisions for prisoner releases, CNDP's transformation into a political party, refugee reintegration, and the incorporation of CNDP members into government institutions and the national army.<ref name=":411">{{Cite web |date=4 December 2012 |title=Rapport synthese des violations des droits de l'homme commises par le Mouvement dit M23 dans la province du Nord-Kivu: Vers la sacralisation des crimes et de l'impunite en R.D. Congo |trans-title=Summary report of human rights violations committed by the M23 Movement in the province of North Kivu: Towards the sacralization of crimes and impunity in the DRC |url=http://iccwomen.org/documents/Rapport-RENADHOC.pdf |access-date=20 February 2025 |website=Iccwomen.org |publisher=Réseau National des ONG des Droits de l'Homme de la République Démocratique du Congo (RENADHOC) |pages=9–15 |language=fr |publication-place=Kinshasa, Democratic Republic of the Congo}}</ref> However, resistance from local communities—who viewed CNDP leaders as perpetrators of crimes—hindered the full implementation of these measures.<ref name=":411" /> Ex-CNDP fighters within the military were accused of exploiting their positions for illegal mineral trade. The rebellion formally commenced on 6 May 2012, culminating in clashes with the Congolese army and the [[MONUSCO|United Nations Organization Stabilization Mission in the Democratic Republic of the Congo]] (MONUSCO).<ref name=":411" /> Both the CNDP as well as the 23 March Movement's first rebellion were supported by Rwanda and Uganda.<ref>{{Cite web |date=12 December 2008 |title=Final report of the Group of Experts on the Democratic Republic of the Congo |url=https://www.securitycouncilreport.org/atf/cf/%7B65BFCF9B-6D27-4E9C-8CD3-CF6E4FF96FF9%7D/DRC%20S%202008%20773.pdf |access-date=20 February 2025 |website=Securitycouncilreport.org |publisher=[[United Nations Security Council]] |pages=15–18}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Jones |first1=Pete |last2=Smith |first2=David |date=20 June 2012 |title=UN report on Rwanda fuelling Congo conflict 'blocked by US' |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2012/jun/20/rwanda-congo-conflict-blocked-us |access-date=21 February 2025 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref name="Schwikowski" /> [[Human Rights Watch]] accused M23 of committing war crimes, including [[summary execution]]s, [[Sexual violence in the Democratic Republic of the Congo|sexual violence]], and forced recruitment, and suggested that some Rwandan officials were complicit due to their continued support for M23 operations.<ref name=":411" /> The uprising was defeated by a joint campaign of the DRC and MONUSCO force. After agreeing to a peace deal, M23 was largely dismantled, its fighters disarmed and moved into [[refugee camps]] in [[Uganda]].<ref name="BBC 30 March" />

Despite the agreement, hostilities between M23 and the DR Congo continued. In 2017, M23 commander [[Sultani Makenga]] and about 100 to 200 of his followers fled from Uganda to resume their insurgency, setting up camp at [[Mount Mikeno]] in the border area between Rwanda, Uganda, and the DR Congo.<ref name="schlindwein" />{{sfn|ICG|2022|p=10}} On 7 November 2021, Makenga's troops carried out a small-scale attack on FARDC positions in Ndiza, Cyanzu, and Runyoni, resulting in four fatalities.<ref name="auto1"/> However, this offensive had little impact, as M23 no longer received significant international support. That same month, Uganda and the DRC had greatly improved their relations, cooperating against a common enemy, the [[Allied Democratic Forces]],<ref name="schlindwein" /> during [[Operation Shujaa]].<ref name="monitor2" /> In early 2022, a growing number of M23 combatants began leaving their camps and moving back to the DRC;<ref name="BBC 30 March" /> the rebel movement launched more attacks in February 2022, but these were repelled.<ref name="schlindwein" /> The M23 leadership argued that parts of their movement had resumed the insurgency because the conditions of the 2013 peace deal were not being honored by the DRC government.<ref name="BBC 30 March" /><ref name="Schwikowski" /> The rebels also argued that they were attempting to defend Kivu's [[Tutsi]] minority from attacks by [[Democratic Forces for the Liberation of Rwanda]] (FDLR),<ref name="africa news 23 May" /> although critics pointed out that the FDLR no longer posed a substantial threat and that M23 operations had commenced before FARDC-FDLR cooperation intensified.<ref name="Bashi2022">{{Cite web |last=Bashi |first=Wendy |date=14 February 2022 |title=Quelle capacité de nuisance des FDLR basées en RDC? |trans-title=What is the nuisance capacity of the FDLR based in the DRC? |url=https://www.dw.com/fr/quelle-capacit%C3%A9-de-nuisance-des-fdlr-bas%C3%A9es-en-rdc/a-60776565 |access-date=17 February 2025 |website=[[DW News]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref name="StearnsAug2022"/><ref name=":622"/> Meanwhile, Ugandan authorities accused Rwanda of reigniting M23's insurgency after its economic interests in the DRC were disrupted.<ref name=":622"/> Earlier, in June 2021, Presidents [[Paul Kagame]] and [[Félix Tshisekedi]] signed an agreement permitting Dither Ltd—allegedly linked to the Rwandan military—to refine gold extracted by [[Société Aurifère du Kivu et du Maniema|Sakima]].<ref name=":622"/> This deal was seen as an effort to cut off funding for armed groups profiting from mineral smuggling. However, as allegations of Rwandan backing for M23 grew, the DRC suspended the agreement in early June 2022.<ref name=":622"/>

The situation was further complicated by the factionalism within M23, as the movement was split into rival groups, namely the "Alliance for the Salvation of the People" headed by [[Jean-Marie Runiga]], and the "Revolutionary Army of Congo" of [[Bertrand Bisimwa]] respectively.<ref name="Karuhanga" /><ref name="politico">{{cite web |url=https://www.politico.cd/la-rdc-a-la-une/2022/06/22/rdc-recrutement-rearmement-et-reorganisation-du-m23-louganda-et-le-rwanda-ont-servi-de-base-arriere.html/110975/ |title=RDC: Recrutement, réarmement et réorganisation du M23, l'Ouganda et le Rwanda ont servi de base arrière |work=politico.cd |date=22 June 2022 |access-date=7 July 2022 |archive-date=11 July 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220711120943/https://www.politico.cd/la-rdc-a-la-une/2022/06/22/rdc-recrutement-rearmement-et-reorganisation-du-m23-louganda-et-le-rwanda-ont-servi-de-base-arriere.html/110975/ |url-status=live |last1=Kinshasa |first1=Rédaction}}</ref> In addition, [[Sultani Makenga|Makenga's]] group was ''de facto'' separate from the other M23 forces which were still mainly based in Uganda.{{sfn|ICG|2022|pp=10, 15}} Later research organized by the [[United Nations Security Council]] suggested that Makenga's return to an insurgency had started the gradual rearmament and restoration of M23, with Bisimwa's "Revolutionary Army of Congo" joining these efforts in late 2021 by reorganizing its remaining fighters and recruiting new ones in cooperation with Makenga. The headquarters of the restored M23 is believed to be located at [[Mount Sabyinyo]].<ref name="politico" />

By 2022, M23 was just one of 120 armed groups that operated in the eastern DR Congo.<ref name="monitor2" /> Before March 2022, the Congolese government made attempts to reinforce its position against the resurgent M23 by sending more troops. However, such measures weakened its presence in other areas, such as those affected by the [[Allied Democratic Forces insurgency]].<ref name="politico" />

== Offensive ==
=== Initial actions ===
In the night of 27 March 2022, M23 rebels launched a new offensive in [[North Kivu]],<ref name="Etahoben2">{{cite news |title=5 DR Congo Chiefs Flee Chiefdoms, Take Refuge In Goma After Persistent M23 Rebel Attacks |url=https://humanglemedia.com/5-dr-congo-chiefs-flee-chiefdoms-take-refuge-in-goma-after-persistent-m23-rebels-attacks/ |author=Bisong Etahoben |work=HumAngle |date=31 March 2022 |access-date=13 June 2022 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613134754/https://humanglemedia.com/5-dr-congo-chiefs-flee-chiefdoms-take-refuge-in-goma-after-persistent-m23-rebels-attacks/ |url-status=live}}</ref> first attacking the villages of Tshanzu and Runyoni in the [[Rutshuru Territory]]<ref name="aljazeera">{{cite news |title=M23 rebels attack military positions in eastern DR Congo |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/3/28/m23-rebels-attack-military-positions-in-eastern-dr-congo |work=Al Jazeera |date=28 March 2022 |access-date=13 June 2022 |archive-date=12 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220612074212/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/3/28/m23-rebels-attack-military-positions-in-eastern-dr-congo |url-status=live}}</ref> from their strongholds at the surrounding hills.<ref name="Schwikowski" /> The two villages had been important strongholds of the M23 Movement during the 2012–13 rebellion.<ref name="aljazeera" /> The rebel attack was reportedly led by Sultani Makenga.<ref name="schlindwein" /> The DRC government claimed that [[Rwanda]] supported the insurgent operation, a claim which was denied by the Rwandan government<ref name="BBC 30 March" /> and the rebels.<ref name="Schwikowski" />

On 29 March, the FARDC was able to repel a rebel attack against the border town of [[Bunagana, Democratic Republic of the Congo|Bunagana]], but M23 captured several villages, including Mugingo, Gasiza, Chengerero, Rugamba, Kibote, Baseke and Kabindi.<ref name="Etahoben2" /> Also, a UN helicopter [[2022 MONUSCO helicopter crash|crashed at Tshanzu]], killing eight MONUSCO peacekeepers. The FARDC blamed M23 rebels for shooting down the aircraft.<ref name="BBC 30 March" />{{sfn|ICG|2022|p=11}} At Bunagana, the FARDC received support by the [[Uganda People's Defence Force]] (UPDF). UPDF ground forces crossed the border, while Ugandan aircraft bombed the rebels.<ref name="schlindwein" /><ref name="monitor3" /> By 1 April, the clashes at Rutshuru had displaced 46,000 locals according to [[United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees|UNHCR]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Thousands Fleeing Violent Clashes in DRC in Need of Emergency Aid |url=https://www.voanews.com/a/thousands-fleeing-violent-clashes-in-drc-in-need-of-emergency-aid/6511405.html |author=Lisa Schlein |work=VOA |date=1 April 2022 |access-date=13 June 2022 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613123222/https://www.voanews.com/a/thousands-fleeing-violent-clashes-in-drc-in-need-of-emergency-aid/6511405.html |url-status=live}}</ref> Meanwhile, the M23 fighters temporarily retreated to their mountain bases, with their first attacks being regarded as a failure.<ref name="schlindwein" /> They subsequently proclaimed a unilateral ceasefire.{{sfn|ICG|2022|p=11}}<ref name="Etahoben3">{{cite news |title=M23 Rebels Withdraw From Villages Captured In DR Congo |url=https://humanglemedia.com/m23-rebels-withdraw-from-villages-captured-in-dr-congo/ |first=Bisong |last=Etahoben |work=HumAngle |date=11 April 2022 |access-date=13 June 2022 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613134752/https://humanglemedia.com/m23-rebels-withdraw-from-villages-captured-in-dr-congo/ |url-status=live}}</ref> One ex-M23 officer told the newspaper ''[[Die Tageszeitung|taz]]'' that it was entirely unclear what the rebel offensive was trying to achieve, with him speculating that Makenga was possibly hoping for one last battle to die in his homeland.<ref name="schlindwein" />

On 6 April, the FARDC rejected any negotiations with the M23 forces based in the DR Congo, and started a counter-attack.{{sfn|ICG|2022|p=11}} Four days later, M23 announced that it would withdraw its troops from any villages captured during the earlier clashes.<ref name="Etahoben3" /> However, as the fighting raged on, FARDC increasingly lost ground to the insurgents.{{sfn|ICG|2022|p=11}} Following the failure of initial peace talks, M23 forces, reportedly led by Makenga<ref name="HRW" /> and including the Bisimwa faction,<ref name="Karuhanga"/> restarted their offensive in May.<ref name="HRW" /> This operation was reportedly supported by at least 1,000 Rwandan soldiers.<ref name="VOA aiding" /> According to a local, M23 overran [[Kibumba]] on 18 May.<ref name="reuters 26 May" /> On 19 May, M23 rebels attacked MONUSCO peacekeepers at [[Shangi, Democratic Republic of the Congo|Shangi]], Rutshuru Territory, as the latter joined the FARDC in counter-insurgency operations. The rebel leadership declared that the attack was in response to a previous joint FARDC-FDLR operation.<ref name="africa news 23 May" />{{efn|It has been reported that some FARDC soldiers who operate in North Kivu are former FDLR members.<ref name="Etahoben" /> A later report for the United Nations by independent researchers stated that the FDLR had begun to fight alongside the FARDC in May, responding to the M23 offensive.<ref name="VOA aiding" />}} From 22 May, the rebels attempted to advance on North Kivu's provincial capital, [[Goma]],<ref name="HRW" /> displacing 70,000 people.<ref name="yusuf">{{cite news |title=DRC Army: M23 Rebels Kill Two Congo Soldiers as Fighting Resumes |url=https://www.voanews.com/a/drc-army-m23-rebels-kill-two-congo-soldiers-as-fighting-resumes/6605739.html |author=Mohammed Yusuf |work=VOA |date=31 May 2022 |access-date=13 June 2022 |archive-date=12 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220612160709/https://www.voanews.com/a/drc-army-m23-rebels-kill-two-congo-soldiers-as-fighting-resumes/6605739.html |url-status=live}}</ref> From 22 to 23 May, a battle raged at Kibumba, while the insurgents temporarily seized [[Rumangabo]] before it was retaken by the FARDC.<ref name="reuters 26 May" /> According to independent researchers, the insurgents were supported by Rwandan soldiers during the battle for Rumangabo.<ref name="VOA aiding" />

On 25 May, M23 reached the outskirts of Goma<ref name="HRW" /> but were repelled by MONUSCO, FARDC,<ref name="yusuf" /> and the FDLR<ref name="VOA aiding" /> after heavy fighting. The insurgents subsequently retreated, and there was a pause in fighting for the rest of the month.<ref name="yusuf" /> North Kivu's deputy police commander, Francois-Xavier Aba van Ang, released a video urging civilians to organize as militiamen to combat M23 in a "people's war".<ref name="HRW" /> The FARDC also began arming existing local militias so that they could assist in the campaign against M23.<ref name="VOA aiding" />

=== 2022–2024 offensives ===
==== Fall of Bunagana ====
[[File:M23offensive.png|left|thumb|250px|Map of the initial [[March 23 Movement|M23]] offensive]]

By early June, M23 reorganized and assaulted a MONUSCO force at Muhati on June 8<ref name="monitor" /> before turning toward Bunagana again.<ref name="reuters voa" /><ref name="yusuf" /> Unlike the previous attack on Bunagana, Ugandan security forces did not intervene and instead retreated from the hills overlooking the town.<ref name="monitor3" /> M23 captured Bunagana on the following day, forcing the local garrison of 137 soldiers and 37 police officers to retreat across the border into Uganda.<ref name="reuters 13 June">{{cite news |title=Congo rebels seize eastern border town, local activists say |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/congo-rebels-seize-key-eastern-border-town-local-activists-say-2022-06-13/ |author=Djaffar Sabiti |work=Reuters |date=13 June 2022 |access-date=13 June 2022 |archive-date=13 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220613090427/https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/congo-rebels-seize-key-eastern-border-town-local-activists-say-2022-06-13/ |url-status=live |url-access=limited}}</ref><ref name="monitor2" /><ref>{{cite news |title=M23 Rebels Reclaim DR Congo Region After Military Takeover |url=https://humanglemedia.com/m23-rebels-reclaim-dr-congo-region-after-military-takeover/ |author=Bisong Etahoben |work=HumAngle |date=14 April 2022 |access-date=13 June 2022 |archive-date=14 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220614114542/https://humanglemedia.com/m23-rebels-reclaim-dr-congo-region-after-military-takeover/ |url-status=live}}</ref> There, they surrendered to the local Ugandan security forces.<ref name="monitor2" /> North Kivu's military governor [[Constant Ndima Kongba]] initially denied that the FARDC had lost the town,<ref name="reuters 13 June" /> but the FARDC spokesman Sylvain Ekenge later declared that the fall of Bunagana constituted "no less than an invasion" by Rwanda.<ref>{{cite news |title=Congo military accuses Rwanda of invasion; rebels seize town |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/m23-rebels-in-eastern-congo-seize-town-near-uganda-border/2022/06/13/3f38b9da-eb33-11ec-9f90-79df1fb28296_story.html |author1=Al-Hadji Kudra Maliro |author2=Justin Kabumba |newspaper=Washington Post |date=13 June 2022 |access-date=13 June 2022 |archive-date=9 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220809221513/https://www.washingtonpost.com/ |url-status=live |url-access=limited}}</ref> In response, the Congolese government suspended "all agreements" with Rwanda.<ref name="monitor4" /> It also accused Uganda of allowing M23 to take over the town and terminated its military cooperation with it.<ref name="monitor3" /> The Ugandan government retaliated by halting Operation Shujaa.<ref name="monitor4">{{cite news |title=Uganda halts Congo military operations |url=https://www.monitor.co.ug/uganda/news/national/uganda-halts-congo-military-operations-3851330 |author=Robert Muhereza |work=Daily Monitor |date=14 June 2022 |access-date=15 June 2022 |archive-date=17 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220617080609/https://www.monitor.co.ug/uganda/news/national/uganda-halts-congo-military-operations-3851330 |url-status=live}}</ref>

MONUSCO began to prepare its local troops to support the efforts of the Congolese security forces to retake the town,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/15/actualite/securite/rdc-les-troupes-de-la-monusco-en-position-defensive-pres-de-bunagana |title=RDC: les troupes de la MONUSCO "en position défensive" près de Bunagana |work=Radio Okapi |date=15 June 2022 |access-date=17 June 2022 |archive-date=16 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220616200716/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/15/actualite/securite/rdc-les-troupes-de-la-monusco-en-position-defensive-pres-de-bunagana |url-status=live}}</ref> while FARDC troops launched a counterattack from Kabindi on 16 June,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/16/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-les-fardc-lancent-lassaut-contre-le-m23-pres-de-bunagana |title=Nord-Kivu: les FARDC lancent l'assaut contre le M23 près de Bunagana |work=Radio Okapi |date=16 June 2022 |access-date=17 June 2022 |archive-date=16 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220616231222/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/16/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-les-fardc-lancent-lassaut-contre-le-m23-pres-de-bunagana |url-status=live}}</ref> and later claimed that they had recaptured Bunagana.<ref>{{Cite news |title=Rutshuru: les FARDC reprennent le contrôlent de la cité de Bunagana |trans-title=Rutshuru: the FARDC regains control of the city of Bunagana |language=fr |url=https://www.agoragrandslacs.net/rutshuru-bunagana-est-sous-controle-des-fardc/ |work=Agora Grands-Lacs |date=16 June 2022 |access-date=29 March 2025 |archive-date=19 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220619130443/https://www.agoragrandslacs.net/rutshuru-bunagana-est-sous-controle-des-fardc/ |url-status=live}}</ref> However, the city was still in rebel hands on the following day, with heavy fighting taking place to its west.<ref name="monitor3" /><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/17/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-reprise-des-combats-entre-fardc-et-m23-autour-de-bunagana |title=Nord-Kivu: reprise des combats entre FARDC et M23 autour de Bunagana |date=17 June 2022 |work=Radio Okapi |access-date=17 June 2022 |archive-date=17 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220617115955/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/17/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-reprise-des-combats-entre-fardc-et-m23-autour-de-bunagana |url-status=live}}</ref> M23 repelled the attack and advanced, capturing the town of Tshengerero<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Sabiti |first1=Djaffar |last2=Bujakera |first2=Stanis |date=17 June 2022 |title=Congo soldier shot dead in Rwanda, rebels make gains in east |language=en |work=National Post |url=https://nationalpost.com/pmn/news-pmn/congo-soldier-shot-dead-in-rwanda-rebels-make-gains-in-east |access-date=17 June 2022}}</ref> and the villages of Bugusa, Kabindi and Rangira.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/17/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-la-situation-militaire-demeure-tendue-jomba |title=Nord-Kivu: la situation militaire demeure tendue à Jomba |date=17 June 2022 |work=Radio Okapi |access-date=17 June 2022 |archive-date=17 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220617172441/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/17/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-la-situation-militaire-demeure-tendue-jomba |url-status=live}}</ref> By June 18, the frontline had stabilized along the Rutshuru-Bunagana axis. Combined FARDC-MONUSCO forces still held settlements in the direct vicinity of Tshengerero such as Ntamugenga and Rwanguba, including the latter's bridge over the Rutshuru river.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/18/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-calme-precaire-rutshuru-sur-la-ligne-de-front-entre-larmee |title=Nord-Kivu: Calme précaire à Rutshuru sur la ligne de front entre l'armée et le M23 |work=Radio Okapi |date=18 June 2022 |access-date=20 June 2022 |archive-date=19 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220619202228/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/18/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-calme-precaire-rutshuru-sur-la-ligne-de-front-entre-larmee |url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="RadioOkapi">{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/20/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-nouveaux-affrontements-entre-fardc-et-m23-dans-le-groupement |title=Nord-Kivu: nouveaux affrontements entre FARDC et M23 dans le groupement Bweza |work=Radio Okapi |date=20 June 2022 |access-date=20 June 2022 |archive-date=20 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220620080503/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/20/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-nouveaux-affrontements-entre-fardc-et-m23-dans-le-groupement |url-status=live}}</ref> Fighting shifted to the Runyoni-Rumangabo axis, where clashes were reported at the villages of Kavumu and Bikenge.<ref name="RadioOkapi" /> As its offensive moved westward, M23 reopened the Bunagana border post and began developing its own parallel administration in the town.<ref>{{cite news |title=DR Congo rebels reopen border post with Uganda |url=https://www.monitor.co.ug/uganda/news/national/dr-congo-rebels-reopen-border-post-with-uganda-3854222 |agency=AFP |work=Daily Monitor |date=20 June 2022 |access-date=22 June 2022 |archive-date=21 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220621180932/https://www.monitor.co.ug/uganda/news/national/dr-congo-rebels-reopen-border-post-with-uganda-3854222 |url-status=live}}</ref>

==== Rutshuru front ====

[[File:PHOTO DU JOUR DU SAMEDI 3 JUILLET 2022.jpg|thumb|MONUSCO peacekeepers on patrol in [[Rutshuru Territory]], July 2022 |232x232px]]

From 19 to 22 June, clashes continued as M23 attempted to break through FARDC defense positions. At first, the rebels assaulted villages along the southern axis, but were repelled at Karambi, Kitagoma and Kitovu, Bweza, and Busanza. They subsequently focused on Bikenge, Ruvumu, Shangi, and Bukima, overrunning the villages before the FARDC organized a counterattack, retaking most of these settlements.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/21/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-les-combats-sintensifient-entre-les-fardc-et-m23-rutshuru |title=Nord-Kivu: les combats s'intensifient entre les FARDC et M23 à Rutshuru |trans-title=North Kivu: fighting intensifies between the FARDC and M23 in Rutshuru |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=21 June 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=22 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220622094946/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/21/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-les-combats-sintensifient-entre-les-fardc-et-m23-rutshuru |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/22/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-13-civils-tues-par-les-bombes-lancees-par-le-m23-kisigari |title=Nord-Kivu: 13 civils tués par les bombes lancées par le M23 à Kisigari |trans-title=North Kivu: 13 civilians killed by M23 bombs in Kisigari |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=22 June 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=23 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220623065500/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/22/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-13-civils-tues-par-les-bombes-lancees-par-le-m23-kisigari |url-status=live}}</ref> [[Human Rights Watch]] reported that 17 civilians, including two children, had been [[summarily executed]] by M23 on 21 June for suspected collaboration with the FARDC.<ref>{{cite web |date=25 July 2022 |title=DR Congo: Resurgent M23 Rebels Target Civilians |url=https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/07/25/dr-congo-resurgent-m23-rebels-target-civilians |access-date=25 July 2022 |website=Human Rights Watch |archive-date=25 July 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220725040402/https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/07/25/dr-congo-resurgent-m23-rebels-target-civilians |url-status=live}}</ref> According to the newspaper ''Eco News'', the FARDC reportedly inflicted a defeat on M23 at the Runyoni frontline around this time, wounding Sultani Makenga and killing another rebel commander, Colonel [[Yusuf Mboneza]].<ref name="RadioOkapi2" /> Mboneza's death was later disputed by pro-M23 sources.<ref name="umuseke" /> Combat resumed on 28 June as rebels attacked FARDC positions at Bushandaba, Ruseke and the strategic hill of Bikona.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/28/actualite/securite/rdc-nouveaux-affrontements-entre-larmee-et-le-m23-rutshuru |title=RDC: nouveaux affrontements entre l'armée et le M23 à Rutshuru |trans-title=DRC: new clashes between the army and the M23 in Rutshuru |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=28 June 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=29 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220629162210/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/28/actualite/securite/rdc-nouveaux-affrontements-entre-larmee-et-le-m23-rutshuru |url-status=live}}</ref> Pro-government forces, consisting of the military and police, counter-attacked, and retook the villages of Nkokwe, Ruvumu, Rugarama, Rutakara, Ntamugenga and Rutsiro.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/29/actualite/securite/rutshuru-les-fardc-reprennent-le-controle-de-4-villages-jadis-occupes |title=Rutshuru: les FARDC reprennent le contrôle de 4 villages jadis occupés par le M23 |trans-title=Rutshuru: the FARDC regains control of 4 villages formerly occupied by the M23 |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=29 June 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=29 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220629171652/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/29/actualite/securite/rutshuru-les-fardc-reprennent-le-controle-de-4-villages-jadis-occupes |url-status=live}}</ref> On 29 June, the FARDC continued its advance, capturing Kabindi and Chengerero, though M23 militants countered by attacking Rutsiro.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/30/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-deux-villages-de-jomba-sous-controle-des-fardc |title=Nord-Kivu: deux villages de Jomba sous contrôle des FARDC |trans-title=North Kivu: two villages in Jomba under FARDC control |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=30 June 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=30 June 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220630123409/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/06/30/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-deux-villages-de-jomba-sous-controle-des-fardc |url-status=live}}</ref>

On July 1, the FARDC claimed to had won a major victory over M23 and allied Rwandan troops after heavy fighting at Rutsiro, Ntamugenga and Nyabikona,<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/01/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-les-fardc-affirment-avoir-neutralise-27-rebelles-m23 |title=Nord-Kivu: les FARDC affirment avoir "neutralisé 27 rebelles M23" à Rutshuru |trans-title=North Kivu: FARDC claims to have "neutralized 27 M23 rebels" in Rutshuru |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=1 July 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=7 July 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220707133908/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/01/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-les-fardc-affirment-avoir-neutralise-27-rebelles-m23 |url-status=live}}</ref> completely evicting the insurgents from the Bweza [[Groupings of the Democratic Republic of the Congo|groupement]] in Rutshuru.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/04/actualite/securite/rdc-reprise-des-combats-entre-les-fardc-et-le-m23-kisigari |title=RDC: reprise des combats entre les FARDC et le M23 à Kisigari |trans-title=DRC: Renewed fighting between the FARDC and the M23 in Kisigari |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=4 July 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=6 July 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220706112029/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/04/actualite/securite/rdc-reprise-des-combats-entre-les-fardc-et-le-m23-kisigari |url-status=live}}</ref> Clashes continued at Bikenge and Ruseke on 4 July, as the FARDC repelled M23 assaults.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/04/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-calme-precaire-dans-les-zones-de-bikenke-et-ruseke |title=Nord-Kivu: calme précaire dans les zones de Bikenke et Ruseke |trans-title=North Kivu: Precarious calm in the areas of Bikenke and Ruseke |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=4 July 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=6 July 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220706161228/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/04/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-calme-precaire-dans-les-zones-de-bikenke-et-ruseke |url-status=live}}</ref> On July 6 Rwandan President [[Paul Kagame]] and  Congolese President Félix Tshisekedi agreed to a ceasefire but M23 refused to adhere to it and attacked Kanyabusoro and Rwanguba the next day.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/07/actualite/securite/rdc-reprise-des-combats-entre-les-fardc-et-le-m23 |title=RDC: reprise des combats entre les FARDC et le M23 |trans-title=DRC: Renewed fighting between the FARDC and the M23 |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=7 July 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=7 July 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220707133918/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/07/actualite/securite/rdc-reprise-des-combats-entre-les-fardc-et-le-m23 |url-status=live}}</ref> Over the next days, clashes continued at various villages in the Bweza and Jomba groupements, as M23 attempted to retake territory<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/08/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-poursuite-des-combats-entre-fardc-et-m23-ntamugenga |title=Nord-Kivu: poursuite des combats entre FARDC et M23 à Ntamugenga |trans-title=North Kivu: Fighting continues between FARDC and M23 in Ntamugenga |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=8 July 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=8 July 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220708225512/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/08/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-poursuite-des-combats-entre-fardc-et-m23-ntamugenga |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/09/actualite/securite/combats-fardc-m23-environ-2-600-menages-des-deplaces-recenses-au |title=Combats FARDC-M23: environ 2 600 ménages des déplacés recensés au village Buhiri |trans-title=FARDC-M23 fighting: about 2,600 displaced households identified in Buhiri village |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=9 July 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=9 July 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220709140139/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/09/actualite/securite/combats-fardc-m23-environ-2-600-menages-des-deplaces-recenses-au |url-status=live}}</ref> while combat died down along other parts of the frontline.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/10/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-afflux-de-deplaces-nyirangongo-la-suite-des-combats-entre |title=Nord-Kivu: afflux de déplacés à Nyirangongo à la suite des combats entre FARDC et M23 |trans-title=North Kivu: Influx of displaced people in Nyirangongo following fighting between FARDC and M23 |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=10 July 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=10 July 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220710114324/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/10/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-afflux-de-deplaces-nyirangongo-la-suite-des-combats-entre |url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:Anti-MONUSCO protests in Goma in July 2022.png|thumb|left|[[Anti-MONUSCO protests]] in [[Goma]], July 2022]]

By the end of July, M23 was installing its own officials in the occupied territories and began raising taxes.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/26/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-le-m23-installe-une-administration-parallele-jomba |title=Nord-Kivu: le M23 installe une administration parallèle à Jomba |trans-title=North Kivu: M23 installs parallel administration in Jomba |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=26 July 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=3 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220803164043/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/26/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-le-m23-installe-une-administration-parallele-jomba |url-status=live}}</ref> In response to M23's advances, [[Anti-MONUSCO protests|violent protests]] erupted in Goma and other eastern Congolese cities against MONUSCO, killing several people, including peacekeepers.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/7/26/un-peacekeepers-troops-shoot-two-protesters-dead-in-goma-drc |title=At Least 15 Killed as Anti-UN Protests Spread in Eastern DR Congo |language=en |work=[[Al Jazeera Media Network|Al Jazeera]] |date=26 July 2022 |access-date=27 July 2022 |archive-date=26 July 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220726212051/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/7/26/un-peacekeepers-troops-shoot-two-protesters-dead-in-goma-drc |url-status=live}}</ref> On 27 July, fighting between M23 and FARDC resumed at Kabingo, Rutshuru, as the rebels attempted to harvest the crops planted by locals but were confronted by government forces.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/27/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-reprise-des-combats-entre-fardc-et-m23-rutshuru |title=Nord-Kivu: reprise des combats entre FARDC et M23 à Rutshuru |trans-title=North Kivu: Renewed fighting between FARDC and M23 in Rutshuru |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=27 July 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=3 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220803164051/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/07/27/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-reprise-des-combats-entre-fardc-et-m23-rutshuru |url-status=live}}</ref> On 2 August, the rebels and FARDC fought at five villages in Rutshuru.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/08/03/actualite/securite/affrontements-fardc-m23-au-moins-5-villages-vides-de-leurs-populations |title=Affrontements FARDC-M23: au moins 5 villages vidés de leurs populations à Rutshuru |trans-title=FARDC-M23 clashes: at least 5 villages emptied of their populations in Rutshuru |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=3 August 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=3 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220803163219/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/08/03/actualite/securite/affrontements-fardc-m23-au-moins-5-villages-vides-de-leurs-populations |url-status=live}}</ref> A truce was later agreed, which was condemned by locals in the area for allowing M23 to keep its territory.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/08/11/actualite/societe/rutshuru-la-societe-civile-deplore-la-treve-des-fardc-engagees-aux |title=Rutshuru: la société civile déplore la trêve des FARDC engagées aux combats contre le M23 |trans-title=Rutshuru: civil society deplores the truce of the FARDC engaged in the fight against the M23 |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=11 August 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=19 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220819161402/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/08/11/actualite/societe/rutshuru-la-societe-civile-deplore-la-treve-des-fardc-engagees-aux |url-status=live}}</ref> On 15 August, the first contingent of East African Community peacekeepers arrived in [[Kivu]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/08/16/actualite/securite/mutualisation-des-forces-de-leac-les-groupes-armes-cibles-par-la-force |title=Mutualisation des forces de l'EAC: les groupes armés ciblés par la force militaire régionale |trans-title=Pooling of EAC forces: armed groups targeted by the regional military force |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=16 August 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=19 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220819161357/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/08/16/actualite/securite/mutualisation-des-forces-de-leac-les-groupes-armes-cibles-par-la-force |url-status=live}}</ref> This group, consisting of [[National Defence Force (Burundi)|Burundian soldiers]], pledged to assist in the campaign against M23 and other insurgent factions.<ref name="RadioOkapi5" /> Sporadic clashes restarted on 16 August, when rebels, allegedly supported by Rwandan troops, attacked Rwanguba, Rangira, and Muhibira in Rutshuru.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/08/18/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-larmee-denonce-de-graves-violations-des-droits-humains-lors |title=Nord-Kivu: l'armée dénonce de graves violations des droits humains lors des attaques du M23 le 16 août |trans-title=North Kivu: Army denounces serious human rights violations during M23 attacks on 16 August |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=18 August 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=18 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220818192046/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/08/18/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-larmee-denonce-de-graves-violations-des-droits-humains-lors |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/08/16/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-reprise-des-combats-entre-fardc-et-m23 |title=Nord-Kivu: reprise des combats entre FARDC et M23 |trans-title=North Kivu: renewed fighting between FARDC and M23 |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=18 August 2022 |access-date=30 March 2025 |archive-date=19 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220819161401/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/08/16/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-reprise-des-combats-entre-fardc-et-m23 |url-status=live}}</ref> The M23 leadership claimed that these operations were in response to FARDC aggression, and declared that it wanted a "dialogue" with the government.<ref>{{cite news |title=M23 Rebels Want Dialogue With DR Congo Government |url=https://humanglemedia.com/m23-rebels-want-dialogue-with-dr-congo-government/ |author=Bisong Etahoben |work=HumAngle |date=17 August 2022 |access-date=7 September 2022 |archive-date=7 September 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220907214736/https://humanglemedia.com/m23-rebels-want-dialogue-with-dr-congo-government/ |url-status=live}}</ref> On 19 August, M23 shelled FARDC positions at Jomba, Bweza and Busanza.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/08/19/actualite/securite/rusthuru-le-m23-accuse-de-bombarder-ce-vendredi-les-positions-des |title=Rusthuru: le M23 accusé de bombarder ce vendredi les positions des FARDC à Rwanguba |trans-title=Rutshuru: M23 accused of bombing FARDC positions in Rwanguba |language=fr |work=Radio Okapi |date=19 August 2022 |access-date=19 August 2022 |archive-date=19 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220819103213/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/08/19/actualite/securite/rusthuru-le-m23-accuse-de-bombarder-ce-vendredi-les-positions-des |url-status=live}}</ref>

On October 23, M23 captured the town of [[Ntamugenga]] as they sought to take the strategic RN2 highway. Four civilians were killed and 40 more were injured in the fighting while more than 23,000 people fled their homes.<ref>{{cite news |title=Congo fighting flares as hundreds shelter in convent – army, MSF |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/m23-rebels-clash-with-army-renewed-east-congo-fighting-2022-10-24/ |access-date=30 October 2022 |work=Reuters |date=24 October 2022 |archive-date=29 October 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221029110230/https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/m23-rebels-clash-with-army-renewed-east-congo-fighting-2022-10-24/ |url-status=live |url-access=limited}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=DR Congo troops clash with M23 rebels |url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20221023-dr-congo-troops-clash-with-m23-rebels |access-date=30 October 2022 |agency=France24 |date=23 October 2022 |archive-date=30 October 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221030173143/https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20221023-dr-congo-troops-clash-with-m23-rebels |url-status=live}}</ref> The offensive continued along the RN2 highway, leading to M23 capturing the towns of Rubare, Kalengera, and Kako.<ref>{{cite web |title=DR Congo Army Clashes With M23 Rebels Around Strategic Eastern Highway |url=https://www.thedefensepost.com/2022/10/27/dr-congo-clash-m23-rebels/ |website=The Defense Post |date=27 October 2022 |access-date=30 October 2022 |archive-date=30 October 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221030173146/https://www.thedefensepost.com/2022/10/27/dr-congo-clash-m23-rebels/ |url-status=live}}</ref> On 29 October M23 rebels took control of the towns of [[Rutshuru]] and [[Kiwanja]].<ref>[https://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/official-m23-rebels-capture-towns-eastern-congo-92341297 Rebels makes new advance, and Congo expels Rwandan envoy] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221030103948/https://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/official-m23-rebels-capture-towns-eastern-congo-92341297 |date=30 October 2022}}</ref> Around this time, a Rwandan soldier fighting alongside M23 surrendered to MONUSCO forces.<ref name="Etahoben1">{{cite web |title=Rwandan Soldier Surrenders To UN In DR Congo |url=https://humanglemedia.com/rwandan-soldier-surrenders-to-un-in-dr-congo/ |author=Bisong Etahoben |work=HumAngle |date=7 November 2022 |access-date=7 January 2023 |archive-date=2 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221202135210/https://humanglemedia.com/rwandan-soldier-surrenders-to-un-in-dr-congo/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

Riots broke out in Goma against Rwanda and MONUSCO again on October 31. Several MONUSCO vehicles were burned while the United Nations announced a "strategic and tactical withdrawal" from the [[Rumangabo]] military base to protect its personnel, allowing M23 to occupy it.<ref>{{cite news |title=Protesters in DRC set fire to UN vehicles in Goma |url=https://www.africanews.com/2022/11/02/protesters-set-fire-to-un-vehicles-in-goma-on-tuesday-night-protesting-against-the-presenc/ |access-date=2 November 2022 |agency=africanews |date=2 November 2022 |archive-date=2 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221102084911/http://www.africanews.com/2022/11/02/protesters-set-fire-to-un-vehicles-in-goma-on-tuesday-night-protesting-against-the-presenc/ |url-status=live}}</ref> Kenya subsequently announced that it would send 900 soldiers to fight against M23.<ref name="news24" /> On 7 November, the Congolese military stated they were training 3,000 new recruits to go and fight M23,<ref>{{cite news |title=Congo trains 3,000 new army recruits amid Rwanda tensions |url=https://www.greenwichtime.com/news/article/Congo-trains-3-000-new-army-recruits-amid-Rwanda-17564179.php |access-date=7 November 2022 |agency=greenwich time |date=7 November 2022 |archive-date=7 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221107154607/https://www.greenwichtime.com/news/article/Congo-trains-3-000-new-army-recruits-amid-Rwanda-17564179.php |url-status=live}}</ref> and soon after began to bomb the rebels with fighter jets. Rwanda protested that a [[Air Force of the Democratic Republic of the Congo|Congolese Air Force]] [[Sukhoi Su-25]] had violated its airspace.<ref name="DefensePost2" /> By 15 November 2022, M23 had pushed to the towns of Rugari and Tongo, forcing hundreds of civilians to flee.<ref>{{cite news |title=Thousands displaced as M23 rebels near key DRC city of Goma |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/11/15/hundreds-flee-as-congos-m23-rebels-near-key-city-of-goma |access-date=18 November 2022 |agency=Al Jazeera |date=15 November 2022 |archive-date=18 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221118141701/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/11/15/hundreds-flee-as-congos-m23-rebels-near-key-city-of-goma |url-status=live}}</ref> M23 claimed to have captured the towns of Kibumba, Ruhunda, Buhumba, Kabuhanga, Tongo, and Mulimbi from the FDLR who they accused of working with the Congolese army.<ref>{{cite news |title=M23 claims capture of FDLR strongholds |url=https://www.newtimes.co.rw/article/2723/news/africa/m23-claims-capture-of-fdlr-strongholds |access-date=18 November 2022 |agency=The New Times |date=17 November 2022 |archive-date=18 November 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221118081915/https://www.newtimes.co.rw/article/2723/news/africa/m23-claims-capture-of-fdlr-strongholds |url-status=live}}</ref> By late November, the FARDC had formed a coalition with several local militias, including the FLDR, [[Mai-Mai]] groups, and some Nyatura factions.<ref name="Bahati" />

==== Expansion through North Kivu ====

A MONUSCO investigation documented massacres committed by M23 in November [[Kishishe massacre|killed at least 171 civilians]] in the Tongo and Bambo ''groupements'' of [[Bwito Chiefdom]].<ref name=":1122">{{Cite web |date=8 February 2023 |title=Massacre de civils en RDC par le M23: 171 morts, selon un nouveau bilan de l'ONU |trans-title=Massacre of civilians in the DRC by the M23: 171 dead, according to a new UN report |url=https://information.tv5monde.com/afrique/massacre-de-civils-en-rdc-par-le-m23-171-morts-selon-un-nouveau-bilan-de-lonu-1815201 |access-date=2 February 2025 |website=[[TV5Monde]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref name=":322">{{Cite web |date=17 February 2023 |title=DR Congo: Rwandan-backed M23 rebels perpetrating summary killings and rapes. |url=https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2023/02/dr-congo-rwandan-backed-m23-rebels-perpetrating-summary-killings-and-rapes/ |access-date=26 February 2023 |website=[[Amnesty International]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=6 December 2022 |title=Massacre de Kishishe: le gouvernement de RD Congo évoque un bilan "autour de 300 morts" |trans-title=Kishishe Massacre: DR Congo Government Reports Death Toll "Around 300" |url=https://www.france24.com/fr/afrique/20221206-massacre-de-kishishe-le-gouvernement-de-rd-congo-%C3%A9voque-autour-de-300-morts |access-date=27 July 2023 |website=[[France 24]] |language=fr |publication-place=Paris, France}}</ref> The victims included men, women and children.<ref name=":1122" /><ref name=":322" /> On 23 December, M23 was forced by heavy international pressure to officially hand over Kibumba to the EAC Regional Force. Despite this, their withdrawal was only partial; the insurgents maintained a presence in the town's outskirts. FARDC declared the alleged handover of Kibumba a "sham", intended to distract from M23's attempts to advance in other areas.<ref name="DefensePost" /> On 28 December 2022, [[South Sudan]] sent a contingency of 750 troops to join the East African Contingency to be stationed in Goma.<ref name="VoA South Sudan" /> Over the next weeks, heavy fighting took place between M23 and a number of rival militias allied to FARDC, including [[Alliance of Patriots for a Free and Sovereign Congo|APCLS]] which declared its aim to capture Bwiza from the rebels. Meanwhile, M23 captured several villages and the town of Nyamilima near the Ugandan border.<ref name="DefensePost" />

In January, clashes continued even as M23 declared its intention to surrender the Rumangabo military base to the EAC Regional Force.<ref name="DefensePost" /> On 24 January a Congolese Su-25 was damaged by ground fire from Rwanda after Rwanda said it violated its airspace.<ref>{{cite news |title=Rwanda says Congo fighter jet violated its airspace |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/rwanda-says-congo-fighter-jet-violated-its-airspace-2023-01-24/ |access-date=24 January 2023 |work=Reuters |date=24 January 2022 |archive-date=24 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230124185125/https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/rwanda-says-congo-fighter-jet-violated-its-airspace-2023-01-24/ |url-status=live |url-access=limited}}</ref> On 27 January M23 captured the town of Kitshanga, causing people to flee and take refuge in a nearby UN base.<ref>{{cite news |title=UN condemns M23 rebel offensive on DRC town as hundreds flee |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/1/27/u-n-condemns-m23-rebel-offensive-on-congo-town-hundreds-flee |access-date=27 January 2023 |agency=Al Jazeera |date=27 January 2023 |archive-date=26 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230226042240/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/1/27/u-n-condemns-m23-rebel-offensive-on-congo-town-hundreds-flee |url-status=live}}</ref> Kitshanga's capture cut off the road linking [[Butembo]], North Kivu's second largest city, to Goma.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2 February 2023 |title=Goma Suffocating After M23 Rebels Cut Off Road From Butembo |url=https://taarifa.rw/goma-suffocating-after-m23-rebels-cut-off-road-from-butembo/ |access-date=2 February 2023 |website=Taarifa |archive-date=2 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230202204517/https://taarifa.rw/goma-suffocating-after-m23-rebels-cut-off-road-from-butembo/ |url-status=live}}</ref> After two days of heavy fighting, M23 seized the village of Mushaki on 24 February, threatening supply routes to Goma.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Malkia |first1=Christina |last2=Kabumba |first2=Justin |date=24 February 2023 |title=Rebels capture more ground in Congo as fighting intensifies |url=https://www.newstimes.com/news/world/article/rebels-capture-more-ground-in-congo-as-fighting-17804118.php |access-date=26 February 2023 |website=News Times |archive-date=26 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230226042221/https://www.newstimes.com/news/world/article/rebels-capture-more-ground-in-congo-as-fighting-17804118.php |url-status=live}}</ref> Three days later M23 briefly took the town of Rubaya and its [[Rubaya mines|coltan mines]],<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mulegwa |first=Pascal |date=27 February 2023 |title=Congolese M23 rebels seize major mining town of Rubaya |url=https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/congolese-m23-rebels-seize-major-mining-town-of-rubaya/2832658 |access-date=27 February 2023 |website=Anadolu Agency |archive-date=27 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230227175337/https://www.aa.com.tr/en/africa/congolese-m23-rebels-seize-major-mining-town-of-rubaya/2832658 |url-status=live}}</ref> before being expelled by the FARDC.<ref>{{cite web | title=Nord-Kivu: la société minière SMB pillée systématiquement à Rubaya (avocat) | website=[[Radio Okapi]] | date=2023-02-28 | url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2023/02/28/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-la-societe-miniere-smb-pillee-systematiquement-rubaya-avocat | language=fr | access-date=2023-03-04}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | title=Masisi: les FARDC reprennent la cité de Rubaya | website=[[Radio Okapi]] | date=2023-03-01 | url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2023/03/01/actualite/securite/masisi-les-fardc-reprennent-la-cite-de-rubaya | language=fr | access-date=2023-03-04}}</ref> The next day, the town of Mweso also fell to the rebels.<ref>{{Cite web |date=28 February 2023 |title=Rebel Clashes Flare in East DR Congo Despite Pullout Plan |url=https://www.voanews.com/a/rebel-clashes-flare-in-east-dr-congo-despite-pullout-plan-/6983022.html |access-date=1 March 2023 |website=Voice of America |archive-date=28 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230228231907/https://www.voanews.com/a/rebel-clashes-flare-in-east-dr-congo-despite-pullout-plan-/6983022.html |url-status=live}}</ref> By March, the violence had resulted in 100,000 displaced civilians.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Western Donors Pressed to Sanction Rwanda as DRC Violence Escalates |url=https://www.voanews.com/a/western-donors-pressed-to-sanction-rwanda-as-drc-violence-escalates-/7010244.html |website=VOA |date=17 March 2023 |access-date=13 April 2023 |archive-date=13 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230413195040/https://www.voanews.com/a/western-donors-pressed-to-sanction-rwanda-as-drc-violence-escalates-/7010244.html |url-status=live}}</ref>

On April 3, Ugandan EAC soldiers entered Bunagana. However, instead of replacing M23, the peacekeepers coexisted with the rebels.<ref name="RadioOkapi0423">{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2023/04/04/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-le-contingent-ougandais-de-leac-cohabite-avec-les-rebelles |title=Nord-Kivu: le contingent ougandais de l'EAC cohabite avec les rebelles du M23 à Bunagana (sources locales) |trans-title=North Kivu: the Ugandan contingent of the EAC coexists with the M23 rebels in Bunagana (local sources) |work=Radio Okapi |date=4 April 2023 |access-date=5 May 2025 |archive-date=13 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230413200543/https://www.radiookapi.net/2023/04/04/actualite/securite/nord-kivu-le-contingent-ougandais-de-leac-cohabite-avec-les-rebelles |url-status=live}}</ref> Such an arrangement was also observed at Rumangabo, where Kenyans and M23 inhabited the same base, and along the Sake-Mwesso axis, where Burundian and rebel forces operated next to each other.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2023/04/03/actualite/securite/rdc-arrivee-des-detachements-precurseurs-des-militaires-sud-soudanais |title=RDC: arrivée des détachements précurseurs des militaires sud-soudanais à Goma |trans-title=DRC: Arrival of the precursor detachments of the South Sudanese military in Goma |work=Radio Okapi |date=3 April 2023 |access-date=5 May 2025 |archive-date=13 April 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230413195123/https://www.radiookapi.net/2023/04/03/actualite/securite/rdc-arrivee-des-detachements-precurseurs-des-militaires-sud-soudanais |url-status=live}}</ref> In addition, local sources suggested that M23 had begun to arm and train other militias.<ref name="RadioOkapi0423" /> In October 2023, the DRC ordered the EAC force in the country to leave by 8 December, due to a "lack of satisfactory results on the ground".<ref>{{Cite web |date=10 October 2023 |title=Congo orders regional peacekeepers to leave by December |url=https://apnews.com/article/congo-m23-rebels-east-african-peacekeeping-mission-65e3d72e57be636f1436a11ac29ac578 |access-date=29 October 2023 |website=AP News |language=en |archive-date=29 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029184110/https://apnews.com/article/congo-m23-rebels-east-african-peacekeeping-mission-65e3d72e57be636f1436a11ac29ac578 |url-status=live}}</ref> On 26 October, the M23 rebels launched an offensive on Bambo, seizing the town,<ref>{{Cite web |date=26 October 2023 |title=Democratic Republic of Congo rocked by new M23 rebel offensive |url=https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2023/10/26/democratic-republic-of-congo-rocked-by-new-m23-rebel-offensive_6206156_4.html |access-date=29 October 2023 |website=Le Monde.fr |language=en |archive-date=28 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231028232619/https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2023/10/26/democratic-republic-of-congo-rocked-by-new-m23-rebel-offensive_6206156_4.html |url-status=live}}</ref> while fighting closed in on Goma.<ref>{{Cite web |agency=AFP |title=DR Congo Fighting Moves Close To Goma City |url=https://www.barrons.com/news/dr-congo-fighting-moves-close-to-goma-city-ea599a8f |access-date=29 October 2023 |website=Barron's |language=en-US |archive-date=29 October 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231029183859/https://www.barrons.com/news/dr-congo-fighting-moves-close-to-goma-city-ea599a8f |url-status=live}}</ref> On 4 February 2024, M23 seized the town of Shasha, adjacent settlements, and was in control of the road linking Goma to [[Minova]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ndushabandi |first=Claver |date=5 February 2024 |title=M23 Rebels Seize Strategic Town in Rapid Advance to Goma |url=https://chimpreports.com/m23-rebels-seize-strategic-town-in-rapid-advance-to-goma/ |access-date=27 February 2024 |archive-date=7 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240207124930/https://chimpreports.com/m23-rebels-seize-strategic-town-in-rapid-advance-to-goma/ |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Powell |first=Jim |date=15 March 2024 |title=The week around the world in 20 pictures |url=https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/gallery/2024/mar/15/the-week-around-the-world-in-20-pictures |access-date=16 March 2024 |work=the Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077 |archive-date=16 March 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240316000349/https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/gallery/2024/mar/15/the-week-around-the-world-in-20-pictures |url-status=live}}</ref> The M23 rebels then began advancing on the town of [[Sake, Democratic Republic of the Congo|Sake]], located at a strategic crossroad linking Goma to the west.<ref>{{Cite web |date=7 February 2024 |title=Residents scatter as they fear Congo rebel group will again seize key eastern city of Goma |website=[[Associated Press News]] |url=https://apnews.com/article/congo-north-kivu-ituri-goma-violence-conflict-tshisekedi-0c1990292498e65875cd7f989b65f641 |access-date=27 February 2024 |archive-date=26 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240226143822/https://apnews.com/article/congo-north-kivu-ituri-goma-violence-conflict-tshisekedi-0c1990292498e65875cd7f989b65f641 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ndushabandi |first=Claver |date=7 February 2024 |title=M23 Rebels Advance Toward Sake as Thousands Flee to Goma |url=https://chimpreports.com/m23-rebels-advance-toward-sake-as-thousands-flee-to-goma/ |access-date=27 February 2024 |website=ChimpReports |archive-date=8 February 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240208163512/https://chimpreports.com/m23-rebels-advance-toward-sake-as-thousands-flee-to-goma/ |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== 2025 Goma and Bukavu offensives ===
==== Encirclement of Goma ====
On January 4, M23 seized [[Masisi, Democratic Republic of the Congo|Masisi]], a town with a population of 40,000 and the administrative seat of [[Masisi Territory]]. ''[[Agence France-Presse]]'' said that M23 forces had previously captured the Katale area before entering Masisi.<ref>{{Cite web |agency=AFP |title=M23 Rebels Seize Key Town In East DR Congo: Sources |url=https://www.barrons.com/news/rwanda-backed-m23-rebels-seize-key-town-in-east-dr-congo-sources-4ea8285c |access-date=5 January 2025 |website=www.barrons.com}}</ref> The same day, M23 forces began moving west to retake [[Rubaya mines|Rubaya]], a key area for the extraction of coltan, a mineral vital to global technology supply chains. The FARDC stopped the initial M23 assault on its newly established positions in Ngungu, in the Mupfuni-Shanga ''groupement''.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kombi |first=Jonathan |date=14 January 2025 |title=Masisi: FARDC et M23 s'affrontent autour de Ngungu ce mardi |trans-title=Masisi: FARDC and M23 clash around Ngungu this Tuesday |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/14/masisi-fardc-et-m23-saffrontent-autour-de-ngungu-ce-mardi |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref name=":5">{{Cite web |last=Kombi |first=Jonathan |date=14 January 2025 |title=Rutshuru: combat signalé entre le M23 et les miliciens wazalendo à Tongo |trans-title=Rutshuru: fighting reported between M23 and Wazalendo militiamen in Tongo |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/14/rutshuru-combat-signale-entre-le-m23-et-les-miliciens-wazalendo-tongo |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> FARDC then conducted airstrikes in Mbingi, a region it was expected to control but failed to maintain for more than a day due to M23 reinforcements.<ref name=":6">{{Cite web |last=Mutanava |first=Joshua |date=15 January 2025 |title=Nord-Kivu: Poursuite des combats entre les FARDC et le M23 à Luofu dans le Lubero |trans-title=North Kivu: Fighting continues between the FARDC and the M23 in Luofu in Lubero |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/15/nord-kivu-poursuite-des-combats-entre-les-fardc-et-le-m23-luofu-dans-le-lubero |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> The following day, M23 fighters based in Mulimbi launched an assault on the Wazalendo stronghold within the Tongo ''groupement'' of [[Bwito Chiefdom]]. During an ambush by Wazalendo near Kiseguro, located in the Binza ''groupement'' in Rutshuru Territory, five M23 fighters were killed.<ref name=":5"/><ref name=":7">{{Cite web |last=Kombi |first=Jonathan |date=17 January 2025 |title=Rutshuru: le bilan des civils tués en représailles par le M23 à Kihondo passe de 7 à 9 morts |trans-title=Rutshuru: the number of civilians killed in reprisals by the M23 in Kihondo rises from 7 to 9 dead |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/17/rutshuru-le-bilan-des-civils-tues-en-represailles-par-le-m23-kihondo-passe-de-7-9-morts |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> Heavy fighting lasted several hours after the ambush, particularly in the localities of Kiseguro and Ngwenda, where Wazalendo forces engaged in combat with M23 troops.<ref name=":5" />

On 19 January, M23 captured the mineral-rich towns of Lumbishi and Changue in [[Kalehe Territory]], marking M23's first incursions into [[South Kivu]] province.<ref name=":10">{{Cite web |last=Mutanava |first=Joshua |date=20 January 2025 |title=RDC: nouveaux combats entre les FARDC et le M23 près de Sake |trans-title=DRC: new fighting between the FARDC and the M23 near Sake |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/20/rdc-nouveaux-combats-entre-les-fardc-et-le-m23-pres-de-sake |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> On 20 January, M23 launched a series of bombings targeting hills overlooking Sake in the Kamuronza ''groupement'' of Masisi Territory, which targeted FARDC and Wazalendo positions, primarily affected the locality of Kimoka near the Lushagala refugee camp. Despite the intensity of the attacks, M23 was repelled.<ref name=":10"/> However, later that evening, M23 forces captured [[Minova]] after intense fighting in Masisi Territory. Minova became the first significant town in South Kivu to fall to M23.<ref name=":11">{{Cite web |date=21 January 2025 |title=RDC: le M23 s'empare de la cité de Minova au Sud-Kivu |trans-title=DRC: M23 seizes the city of Minova in South Kivu |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/21/rdc-le-m23-sempare-de-la-cite-de-minova-au-sud-kivu |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> Strategic vantage points such as Katale, Kachiazo, and the hills overlooking Minova were occupied.<ref name=":11"/> M23 forces then moved toward Kasunyu, a route leading to both Goma and Rwanda, effectively cutting off a critical supply route to Goma. The rebels also advanced toward Kalungu, located 7 kilometers from Minova, with the potential to reach Nyabibwe, another mining hub. The axis also pointed toward Kavumu, home to a regional airport.<ref name=":11"/><ref>{{Cite web |work=Reuters |title=Congo rebels seize eastern town on critical supply route |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/congos-m23-rebels-seize-eastern-town-minova-2025-01-21/ |access-date=21 January 2025 |language=en-US |url-access=limited}}</ref> [[Bweremana]] was taken by M23 following an artillery assault against FARDC forces.<ref name=":12">{{Cite web|last=Mutanava|first=Joshua |date=21 January 2025|title=RDC: symbole de la résistance depuis des mois, la localité côtière de Bwerema passe sous contrôle du M23|trans-title=DRC: Symbol of resistance for months, the coastal town of Bwerema comes under the control of the M23|url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/21/rdc-symbole-de-la-resistance-depuis-des-mois-la-localite-cotiere-de-bwerema-passe-sous|access-date=26 January 2025|website=[[Actualite.cd]]|language=fr}}</ref>

By 22 January, M23 had entrenched itself within Minova and Kalungu in the Buzi ''groupement'' and expanded its dominion to Makelele and Butale in the Mbinga-Nord ''groupement''.<ref name=":13">{{Cite web |last=Mwamba |first=Justin |date=22 January 2025 |title=Sud-Kivu: un calme précaire sur différents fronts à Kalehe au lendemain d'intenses combats entre FARDC et M23 |trans-title=South Kivu: Precarious calm on different fronts in Kalehe following intense fighting between FARDC and M23 |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/22/sud-kivu-un-calme-precaire-sur-differents-fronts-kalehe-au-lendemain-dintenses-combats |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> On 23 January, intensified clashes erupted as M23 launched a pre-dawn offensive against FARDC and Wazalendo positions near Sake.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Kombi |first1=Jonathan |last2=Kapinga |first2=Yvonne |date=23 January 2025 |title=RDC: intenses combats entre l'armée et le M23 pour le contrôle de Sake |trans-title=DRC: Intense fighting between the army and the M23 for control of Sake |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/23/rdc-intenses-combats-entre-larmee-et-le-m23-pour-le-controle-de-sake |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> Seven South African soldiers of the SADC were killed in the fighting.<ref name=":14">{{Cite web |date=23 January 2025 |title=RDC: alors que les combats font rage, l'Afrique du Sud dépêche sa ministre de la défense pour visiter la SANDF qui combat le M23 dans l'est |trans-title=DRC: As fighting rages, South Africa dispatches its defense minister to visit the SANDF fighting the M23 in the east |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/23/rdc-alors-que-les-combats-font-rage-lafrique-du-sud-depeche-sa-ministre-de-la-defense |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> MONUSCO mobilized reinforcements to fortify defenses around Sake and Goma but remained absent in Minova due to mandate restrictions.<ref>{{Cite web |date=23 January 2025 |title=RDC: la MONUSCO se mobilise autour de Sake et Goma face au M23, mais absente à Minova (Sud-Kivu) faute de mandat |trans-title=DRC: MONUSCO mobilizes around Sake and Goma against the M23, but absent in Minova (South Kivu) due to lack of mandate |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/23/rdc-la-monusco-se-mobilise-autour-de-sake-et-goma-face-au-m23-mais-absente-minova-sud |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> Simultaneous attacks by M23 targeted Wazalendo and FARDC positions 20 kilometers north of Goma.<ref name=":15">{{Cite web |last=Mutanava |first=Joshua |date=23 January 2025 |title=RDC: Intensification des combats entre l'armée et le M23 dans le territoire de Nyiragongo ce jeudi |trans-title=DRC: Intensification of fighting between the army and the M23 in the territory of Nyiragongo this Thursday |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/23/rdc-intensification-des-combats-entre-larmee-et-le-m23-dans-le-territoire-de-nyiragongo |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref>

By late January, fighting intensified around Sake as M23 launched several assaults to take it. Multiple MONUSCO peacekeepers were wounded defending the area around the town<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 January 2025 |title=RDC: Neuf Casques bleus blessés lors des affrontements avec le M23/RDF |trans-title=DRC: Nine Blue Helmets Injured During Clashes with M23/RDF |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/24/rdc-neuf-casques-bleus-blesses-lors-des-affrontements-avec-le-m23rdf |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> while Major General [[Peter Cirimwami Nkuba]], the military governor of North Kivu, was injured in the fighting and later died of his wounds.<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 January 2025 |title=RDC: Décès confirmé du gouverneur militaire du Nord-Kivu Peter Cirimwami atteint par balle au front contre le M23 |trans-title=DRC: Confirmed death of North Kivu military governor Peter Cirimwami shot in the forehead against M23 |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/24/rdc-deces-confirme-du-gouverneur-militaire-du-nord-kivu-peter-cirimwami-atteint-par |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> Hundreds of civilians became casualties amid the ensuing battle<ref>{{Cite web |date=26 January 2025 |title=RDC: Tirs sporadiques dans certains quartiers de Goma, situation toujours confuse- Le point à 23h (heure locale) |trans-title=DRC: Sporadic shooting in some districts of Goma, situation still confused – Update at 11 p.m. (local time) |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/26/rdc-tirs-sporadiques-dans-certains-quartiers-de-goma-situation-toujours-confuse-le-point |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> along with two [[Malawian Defence Force]] peacekeepers killed defending to town.<ref>{{Cite web |date=25 January 2025 |title=RDC: Trois soldats malawites tués dans des affrontements avec le M23/RDF |trans-title=DRC: Three Malawian soldiers killed in clashes with M23/RDF |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/25/rdc-trois-soldats-malawites-tues-dans-des-affrontements-avec-le-m23rdf |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> Sake was taken by M23 on January 23, rendering Goma encircled.<ref name="ISW">{{Cite web |title=Africa File, January 24, 2025 |website=[[Institute for the Study of War]] |date=24 January 2025 |access-date=28 January 2025 |url=https://understandingwar.org/backgrounder/africa-file-january-24-2025-russia-continues-pivot-libya-and-mali-saf-advances-khartoum }}</ref>  As M23 advanced toward the city, six more United Nations peacekeepers<ref name="SixMON"/><ref>{{Cite web |last=Press |first=Associated |date=25 January 2025 |title=Six peacekeepers killed fighting rebels in eastern Congo, officials say, as rebels close in on Goma |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2025/01/25/africa/un-peacekeepers-drc-congo-m23-afr-intl-latam/index.html#:~:text=Fighting%20with%20M23%20rebels%20in,official%20told%20The%20Associated%20Press. |access-date=5 March 2025 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref> and nine South African soldiers were killed trying to repulse them.<ref>{{Cite web |date=25 January 2025 |title=RDC: neuf soldats sud-africains tués dans les combats contre le M23/RDF, la SANDF annonce avoir stoppé leur avancée |trans-title=DRC: Nine South African soldiers killed in fighting against M23/RDF, SANDF announces having stopped their advance |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/25/rdc-neuf-soldats-sud-africains-tues-dans-les-combats-contre-le-m23rdf-la-sandf-annonce |access-date=26 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref>

==== Battle of Goma ====
{{main|2025 Goma offensive}}

As an M23 assault on Goma became imminent, the FARDC deployed tanks at strategic spots in the city to defend it. At [[Goma International Airport]], Romanian military instructors coordinated with FARDC forces and Wazalendo units to bolster security.<ref name=":21">{{Cite web |date=26 January 2025 |title=RDC: Goma toujours tenu par les FARDC – Situation 19H00 (heure locale) |trans-title=DRC: Goma still held by the FARDC – Situation 7:00 p.m. (local time) |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/26/rdc-goma-toujours-tenu-par-les-fardc-situation-19h00-heure-locale |access-date=27 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> M23 issued an ultimatum demanding the surrender of FARDC forces and imposed a 48-hour deadline, which led to some soldiers relinquishing their arms before the deadline expired.<ref name=":82">{{Cite web|last=Cursino|first=Malu|date=27 January 2025|title=DR Congo conflict: M23 rebels say they have taken eastern city of Goma|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c0qwlkydxxko|access-date=27 January 2025|website=Bbc.com|language=en-GB}}</ref> Some residents fled the city by using boats to cross Lake Kivu.<ref name=":21"/> M23 commenced its attack on the city on January 25, assaulting its northern and western outskirts<ref name="Aikman">{{cite news |last1=Aikman |first1=Ian |last2=Cursino |first2=Malu |title=DR Congo rebels close in on key city as 13 peacekeepers killed |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c2l07e550qko |work=[[BBC News]] |date=26 January 2025 |access-date=26 January 2025}}</ref> while Rwandan forces attacked from Rwanda itself.<ref name="Guardian1">{{Cite news |title=Rwandan army 'ready to invade DRC' and help rebels seize city |newspaper=[[The Guardian]] |last=Townsend |first=Mark |date=25 January 2025 |access-date=27 January 2025 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2025/jan/25/rwandan-army-ready-to-invade-drc-and-help-rebels-seize-city }}</ref> Assisted by MONUSCO, Wazalendo militias, and mercenaries from [[Romania]], the Congolese army put up a stiff resistance fending off M23 in the northern neighborhoods in order to defend the routes leading to Goma's airport.<ref name="Actualite26">{{Cite news |title=RDC : Goma toujours tenu par les FARDC – Situation 19H00 (heure locale) |trans-title=DRC: Goma still held by FARDC – Situation 7 p.m. (local time) |work=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr |date=25 January 2025 |access-date=27 January 2025 |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/26/rdc-goma-toujours-tenu-par-les-fardc-situation-19h00-heure-locale }}</ref> Similar "unexpected resistance" was put up in the west.<ref name="AFP28">{{Cite news |title=After a 'flood of fire', Goma delivered to looters |date=28 January 2025 |access-date=28 January 2025 |work=Yahoo News |agency=[[Agence France-Press]] |url=https://sg.news.yahoo.com/flood-fire-goma-delivered-looters-171100389.html }}</ref> By the next day, M23 had broken through FARDC defenses in the city's Monigi district and began pushing toward the city center.<ref name="ReuJan26">{{Cite news |title=Congo's M23 rebels say they will seize eastern city overnight |date=26 January 2026 |work=[[Reuters]] |access-date=26 January 2025 |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/un-peacekeeper-death-toll-eastern-congo-rises-six-2025-01-26/ }}</ref>

With the city breached, FARDC defenses crumbled. M23 quickly captured strategic neighborhoods around the airport as Congolese soldiers began to flee to peacekeeping bases or into Rwanda where they surrendered.<ref name="AP30">{{Cite news |last1=Asadu |first1=Chinedu |last2=Kabumba |first2=Justin |title=Congo's leader calls for massive military mobilization as Rwanda-backed rebels expand their control |work=[[Associated Press]] |date=30 January 2025 |access-date=30 January 2025 |url=https://apnews.com/article/congo-m23-goma-rwanda-tshisekedi-south-africa-ca81f34f52ea31b85dd845b049a0bb96 }}</ref> On January 27, M23 announced the city's capture but scattered resistance among Wazalendo militias continued in various neighborhoods while remaining FARDC soldiers and MONUSCO peacekeepers held out at the airport which M23 besieged. The next day, the airport was surrendered and M23 took 1,200 FARDC soldiers captive.<ref name="BBC28">{{Cite news |title=Bodies in streets as battle escalates in DR Congo city |date=28 January 2025 |access-date=28 January 2025 |work=[[BBC News]] |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/live/c5y6j6gwgnzt }}</ref> MONUSCO forces promptly raised a [[white flag]] and surrendered afterward.<ref name=":24">{{Cite web |date=28 January 2025 |title=Ramaphosa et Kagame s'accordent sur la nécessité urgente d'un cessez-le-feu, selon la présidence sud-africaine |trans-title=Ramaphosa, Kagame agree on urgent need for ceasefire, says South African presidency |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/28/ramaphosa-et-kagame-saccordent-sur-la-necessite-urgente-dun-cessez-le-feu-selon-la |access-date=28 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> By January 30, fighting had stopped as M23 mopped up any remaining resistance in the city.<ref name="Radio-Okapi30">{{Cite news |title=Situation relativement calme ce jeudi à Goma |trans-title=Relatively calm situation on Thursday in Goma |work=Radio Okapi |language=fr |date=30 January 2025 |access-date=30 January 2025 |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2025/01/30/actualite/securite/situation-relativement-calme-ce-jeudi-goma }}</ref> The United Nations estimated that nearly 3,000 people were killed in the battle.<ref>{{cite news |title=Eastern DRC: Nearly 3,000 dead in Goma offensive, says UN |url=https://www.rfi.fr/fr/afrique/20250204-est-de-la-rdc-pr%C3%A8s-de-3000-morts-dans-l-offensive-sur-goma-d-apr%C3%A8s-l-onu |access-date=5 February 2025 |agency=rfi |date=4 February 2025}}</ref>

==== Push into South Kivu ====
{{see also|2025 Bukavu offensive}}
The city's fall sparked [[2025 Kinshasa riots|large-scale riots]] in Kinshasa. Crowds gathered outside embassies demanding stronger international action and the immediate withdrawal of M23 from the Congo.<ref name=":24"/><ref>{{Cite web|date=28 January 2025|title=Kinshasa: Manifestations devant l'ambassade des États-Unis, écoles fermées, transport perturbé|trans-title= |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/28/kinshasa-manifestations-devant-lambassade-des-etats-unis-ecoles-fermees-transport|access-date=28 January 2025|website=[[Actualite.cd]]|language=fr}}</ref><ref name=":25">{{Cite web |date=28 January 2025 |title=Kinshasa: le Kenya condamne les attaques contre son ambassade et appelle au respect du droit international |trans-title=Kinshasa: Kenya condemns attacks on its embassy and calls for respect for international law |url=https://actualite.cd/index.php/2025/01/28/kinshasa-le-kenya-condamne-les-attaques-contre-son-ambassade-et-appelle-au-respect-du |access-date=28 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Mulegwa |first=Pascal |date=28 January 2025 |title=RDC: violentes manifestations devant des ambassades à Kinshasa |trans-title=DRC: Violent demonstrations in front of embassies in Kinshasa |url=https://www.aa.com.tr/fr/afrique/rdc-violentes-manifestations-devant-des-ambassades-%C3%A0-kinshasa/3464531 |access-date=29 January 2025 |website=Aa.com.tr |language=fr}}</ref> Congolese President Tshisekedi called for a national mobilization, urging citizens to rally behind FARDC against what he called "Rwanda's barbaric aggression".<ref>{{Cite web|last=Mesa|first=Jonathan|date=30 January 2025|title=Agression rwandaise: Félix Tshisekedi lance une mobilisation générale et appelle à un enrôlement massif dans les rangs des FARDC |trans-title=Rwandan aggression: Félix Tshisekedi launches general mobilization and calls for mass enrollment in the ranks of the FARDC|url=https://actualite.cd/index.php/2025/01/30/agression-rwandaise-felix-tshisekedi-lance-une-mobilisation-generale-et-appelle-un|access-date=30 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]]|language=fr}}</ref> With Goma captured, M23 turned its attention to South Kivu and its capital [[Bukavu]], launching an offensive into the province's Kalehe Territory and capturing the towns of Kiniezire and Mukwidja after intense skirmishes.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Institute for the Study of War |url=https://www.understandingwar.org/backgrounder/africa-file-january-30-2025 |access-date=31 January 2025 |website=Institute for the Study of War |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=29 January 2025 |title=RDC: le M23 signalé dans les villages de Kiniezire et de Mukwidja (Kalehe) |trans-title=DRC: M23 reported in the villages of Kiniezire and Mukwidja (Kalehe) |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/29/rdc-le-m23-signale-dans-les-villages-de-kiniezire-et-de-mukwidja-kalehe |access-date=29 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> The next day, M23 confronted FARDC troops in the village of Kalangala. Strategically positioned between Mukwidja and Nyabibwe, it was the gateway to the territory's administrative town of Kalehe.<ref>{{Cite web|date=30 January 2025|title=Sud-Kivu: Violents affrontements entre FARDC et M23 à Kalangala |trans-title=South Kivu: Violent clashes between FARDC and M23 in Kalangala|url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/30/sud-kivu-violents-affrontements-entre-fardc-et-m23-kalangala|access-date=30 January 2025|website=[[Actualite.cd]]|language=fr}}</ref> On January 31, FARDC launched a counterattack, retaking Mukwidja and adjacent localities<ref name=":27">{{Cite web |date=31 January 2025 |title=Sud-Kivu/M23: le village Mukwidja repris par l'armée |trans-title=South Kivu/M23: Mukwidja village retaken by the army |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/01/31/sud-kivum23-le-village-mukwidja-repris-par-larmee |access-date=31 January 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> but clashes persisted in other nearby areas, while FARDC confirmed that clashes had spread beyond Nyabibwe.<ref name=":27"/> The United Nations reported that over 700 people died in the fighting within Kalehe Territory thus far.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hauser |first=Jennifer |date=31 January 2025 |title=UN says more than 700 killed, 2,800 injured in just five days of fighting in DRC |trans-title=UN says more than 700 killed in just five days of fighting in DR Congo |url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/01/31/americas/un-says-more-than-700-killed-in-just-five-days-of-fighting-in-dr-congo/index.html |access-date=1 January 2025 |website=[[CNN]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Lukiv |first=Jaroslav |date=1 January 2025 |title=DR Congo conflict: At least 700 killed since Sunday, says UN |trans-title=At least 700 killed in DR Congo fighting since Sunday – UN |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c78xvgvy172o |access-date=1 February 2025 |website=[[BBC News]] |language=en-GB}}</ref>

On February 1, FARDC retook Nyabibwe and forced M23 to retreat toward Minova but its commanding officer Alexis Rugabisha was killed in action.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2 February 2025 |title=RDC: un haut gradé des FARDC tué dans les combats contre les RDF et le M23, annonce l'armée |trans-title=DRC: High-ranking FARDC officer killed in fighting against RDF and M23, army announces |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/02/02/rdc-un-haut-grade-des-fardc-tue-dans-les-combats-contre-les-rdf-et-le-m23-annonce-larmee |access-date=2 February 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref name=":31">{{Cite web |date=4 February 2025 |title=M23 au Sud-Kivu: le combat est concentré à Nyamasasa, la ville de Bukavu se trouve loin de danger, selon l'ATA de Kalehe |trans-title=M23 in South Kivu: the fight is concentrated in Nyamasasa, the city of Bukavu is far from danger, according to the ATA of Kalehe |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/02/04/m23-au-sud-kivu-le-combat-est-concentre-nyamasasa-la-ville-de-bukavu-se-trouve-loin-de |access-date=5 February 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref name=":32">{{Cite web |date=4 February 2025 |title=Sud-Kivu: les rebelles du M23 se renforcent à Kabugizi et Murambi, alerte la société civile locale |trans-title=South Kivu: M23 rebels gain strength in Kabugizi and Murambi, warns local civil society |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/02/04/sud-kivu-les-rebelles-du-m23-se-renforcent-kabugizi-et-murambi-alerte-la-societe-civile |access-date=5 February 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> Two days later, M23 announced a unilateral ceasefire<ref name=":31"/><ref name=":32"/> but reports indicated that M23 was reinforcing its positions with additional troops and equipment in Kalehe Territory.<ref name=":31" /><ref name=":32"/> On February 4, M23 relaunched its offensive, retaking various villages and the town of Mukwija,<ref name=":32"/> followed by Nyabibwe.<ref name="AP seized">{{Cite news |date=2025-02-05 |title=Rwanda-backed rebels have seized another town in eastern Congo |url=https://apnews.com/article/congo-goma-m23-bukavu-rwanda-871a8bcd404f6579cb83f04f04338a7b |access-date=2025-02-11 |work=Associated Press News |language=en |archive-date=2025-02-10 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250210074950/https://apnews.com/article/congo-goma-m23-bukavu-rwanda-871a8bcd404f6579cb83f04f04338a7b |url-status=live }}</ref> On February 10, M23 launched artillery strikes on FARDC positions,<ref name=":39">{{Cite web |last=Sengenya |first=Claude |date=11 February 2025 |title=RDC: reprise des combats sur le front de Lubero au lendemain d'une attaque meurtrière à la bombe larguée par le M23 |trans-title=DRC: Fighting resumes on the Lubero front the day after a deadly bomb attack dropped by the M23 |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/02/11/rdc-reprise-des-combats-sur-le-front-de-lubero-au-lendemain-dune-attaque-meurtriere-la |access-date=11 February 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref>  while intense fighting in the area forced about 30,000 people to flee their homes.<ref name=":40">{{Cite web |last=Muamba |first=Clement |date=12 February 2025 |title=Sud-Kivu: au moins 30 000 personnes en provenance des villages situés sur le littoral de Minova se sont déplacées et les affrontements à Nyabibwe a suscité de vives inquiétudes parmi les habitants de Bukavu conduisant à la suspension des cours (OCHA) |trans-title=South Kivu: At least 30,000 people from villages on the coast of Minova have been displaced and clashes in Nyabibwe have raised serious concerns among residents of Bukavu leading to the suspension of classes (OCHA) |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/02/12/sud-kivu-au-moins-30-000-personnes-en-provenance-des-villages-situes-sur-le-littoral-de |access-date=12 February 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> M23 captured Kalehe two days later and went on to consolidate control over much of Kalehe Territory.<ref name=":41">{{Cite web |date=12 February 2025 |title=RDC: présence de combattants de l'AFC/M23 à Kalehe-centre et Ihusi, le gouvernement dénonce une violation du cessez-le-feu et accuse ce groupe de recevoir des renforts |trans-title=DRC: Presence of AFC/M23 fighters in Kalehe-centre and Ihusi, the government denounces a violation of the ceasefire and accuses this group of receiving reinforcements |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/02/12/rdc-presence-de-combattants-de-lafcm23-kalehe-centre-et-ihusi-le-gouvernement-denonce |access-date=13 February 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> M23 forces then entered [[Kabare Territory]] on February 13.<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 February 2025 |title=RDC-Kalehe: poursuite des combats ce jeudi entre les FARDC et le M23 dans la localité de Chofi |trans-title=DRC-Kalehe: fighting continues this Thursday between the FARDC and the M23 in the locality of Chofi |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/02/13/rdc-kalehe-poursuite-des-combats-ce-jeudi-entre-les-fardc-et-le-m23-dans-la-localite-de |access-date=13 February 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> On 14 February, M23 occupied all of Kalehe and made inroads into Kabare territories, forcing the Congolese army to withdraw toward the town of Kavumu<ref name=":43">{{Cite web |last=Maki |first=Patrick |date=14 February 2025 |title=RDC: Kabamba et Katana, des entités du territoire de Kabare conquises par les rebelles de l'AFC/M23 |trans-title=DRC: Kabamba and Katana, entities of the Kabare territory conquered by the AFC/M23 rebels |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/02/14/rdc-kabamba-et-katana-des-entites-du-territoire-de-kabare-conquises-par-les-rebelles-de |access-date=15 February 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> where they made a stand at its [[Kavumu Airport|local airport]].<ref name=":43"/> By midday, M23 had successfully overrun the airport, 30 kilometers from Bukavu, despite significant resistance from FARDC.<ref name= ":44">{{Cite web |date=14 February 2025 |title=RDC: situé à une trentaine de kilomètres de Bukavu, l'aéroport de Kavumu passe sous contrôle de la rébellion de l'AFC/M23 |trans-title=DRC: located around thirty kilometers from Bukavu, Kavumu airport comes under the control of the AFC/M23 rebellion |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/02/14/rdc-situe-une-trentaine-de-kilometres-de-bukavu-laeroport-de-kavumu-passe-sous-controle |access-date=15 February 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> M23 then promptly marched on Bukavu on February 15.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Ross |first1=Will |last2=Cursino |first2=Malu |title=DR Congo conflict: M23 rebels enter second major city Bukavu |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cvgmj00zjrpo |work=BBC News |date=15 February 2025}}</ref> Unlike in Goma, M23 faced little resistance taking the city<ref>{{Cite news |date=16 February 2025 |title=Congo says Rwanda-backed rebels occupy a 2nd major city in its mineral-rich east |url=https://apnews.com/article/congo-m23-rebels-bukavu-southkivu-14527185304baa9a5e40eff4cfccba85 |access-date=16 February 2025 |language=en |work=[[Associated Press News]] |archive-date=16 February 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250216223955/https://apnews.com/article/congo-m23-rebels-bukavu-southkivu-14527185304baa9a5e40eff4cfccba85 |url-status=live }}</ref> but its capture forced over 10,000 refugees into Burundi.<ref name=":93">{{Cite web |last=Lukombo |first=Samyr |date=17 February 2025 |title=Occupation de Bukavu par le M23/AFC: Le Burundi dit avoir accueilli environ 10 000 réfugiés congolais fuyant l'avancée des rebelles |trans-title=M23/AFC occupation of Bukavu: Burundi says it has taken in around 10,000 Congolese refugees fleeing rebel advance |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/02/17/occupation-de-bukavu-par-le-m23afc-le-burundi-dit-avoir-accueilli-environ-10-000 |access-date=17 February 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref>

=== Secondary offensives and ceasefire ===

==== Lubero front ====
{{main|2025 Butembo offensive}}

Concurrent with M23's offensives on Goma and Bukavu was a minor theater in [[Lubero Territory]], North Kivu, where they attempted to capture the province's second largest city of [[Butembo]]. Goma's seizure freed up M23's forces to march north where on February 18, they engaged FARDC forces in an attempt to capture the territory's administrative town of [[Lubero]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-02-18 |title=M23 au Nord-Kivu: reprise des hostilités sur plusieurs lignes de Lubero Sud, des déplacements massifs vers Lubero-centre |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/02/18/m23-au-nord-kivu-reprise-des-hostilites-sur-plusieurs-lignes-de-lubero-sud-des |access-date=2025-02-24 |website=Actualite.cd |language=fr}}</ref> M23 captured the town of Kipese on February 20 while flanking FARDC positions along the RN2 highway<ref name="Feb27">{{Cite web |first1=Nick |last1=Markiewicz |first2=Yale |last2=Ford |first3=Liam |last3=Karr |title=Congo War Security Review - February 27, 2025 |date=27 February 2025 |access-date=27 February 2025 |publisher=Critical Threats Project |url=https://www.criticalthreats.org/briefs/congo-war-security-review }}</ref> but was prevented from taking Lubero itself.<ref>{{Cite web |first1=Nick |last1=Markiewicz |first2=Yale |last2=Ford |first3=Liam |last3=Karr |title=Congo War Security Review - February 24, 2025 |date=24 February 2025 |access-date=26 February 2025 |publisher=Critical Threats Project |url=https://www.criticalthreats.org/briefs/congo-war-security-review/congo-war-security-review-february-24-2025 }}</ref> Unable to break FARDC defenses, M23 moved west and captured the village of Kasugho, located southwest of Lubero, on March 2<ref>{{Cite web |first1=Nick |last1=Markiewicz |first2=Yale |last2=Ford |first3=Liam |last3=Karr |title=Congo War Security Review - March 4, 2025 |date=4 March 2025 |access-date=4 March 2025 |publisher=Critical Threats Project |url=https://www.criticalthreats.org/briefs/congo-war-security-review/congo-war-security-review-march-4-2025 }}</ref> while also taking various settlements along the shores of [[Lake Edward]] by May 2.<ref>{{Cite web |first1=Yale |last1=Ford |title=Congo War Security Review - May 5, 2025 |date=5 May 2025 |access-date=27 June 2025 |publisher=Critical Threats Project |url=https://www.criticalthreats.org/briefs/congo-war-security-review/congo-war-security-review-may-5-2025 }}</ref>

In response to M23's advances, Uganda intervened and sent its military into the Congo to secure areas of North Kivu still under government control and neighboring [[Ituri Province]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=The DRC Conflict Enters a Dangerous New Phase |date=26 February 2025 |access-date=1 March 2025 |publisher=Africa Center for Strategic Studies |url=https://africacenter.org/spotlight/drc-conflict-new-phase/ }}</ref> Taking up positions south of Lubero, the Ugandan military stopped M23 from progressing any further.<ref>{{Cite web |first1=Nick |last1=Markiewicz |first2=Yale |last2=Ford |first3=Liam |last3=Karr |title=Congo War Security Review - March 6, 2025 |date=6 March 2025 |access-date=6 March 2025 |publisher=Critical Threats Project |url=https://www.criticalthreats.org/briefs/congo-war-security-review/congo-war-security-review-march-6-2025-67c9f51df3397 }}</ref>

==== Uvira front ====
{{See also|2025 Uvira offensive}}
After securing Bukavu, M23 swiftly marched south along the N5 highway toward the strategic city of [[Uvira]], located near the border with Burundi. FARDC, now suffering from crippling morale, hastily attempted to build new defensive lines but faced resistance from Wazalendo militias who were opposed to abandoning Bukavu.<ref name="uvira1">{{Cite news |last=Rolley |first=Sonia |date=18 February 2025 |title=Congo army retreat from Bukavu leads to clashes with allied militias |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/congo-army-retreat-bukavu-leads-clashes-with-allied-militias-2025-02-18/ |access-date=18 February 2025 |work=Reuters}}</ref> The resulting infighting left 17 people dead on February 19.<ref name=":56">{{Cite web |date=19 February 2025 |title=Affrontements FARDC-Wazalendo à Uvira: une dizaine de morts signalés, la ville plongée dans une psychose généralisée |trans-title=FARDC-Wazalendo clashes in Uvira: around ten deaths reported, the city plunged into general psychosis |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/02/19/affrontements-fardc-wazalendo-uvira-une-dizaine-de-morts-signales-la-ville-plongee-dans |access-date=19 February 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref name=":57">{{Cite web |date=19 February 2025 |title=Situation tendue à Uvira: MSF alerte sur l'urgence de protéger des civils, la mission médicale et l'accès humanitaire |trans-title=Tense situation in Uvira: MSF warns of the urgency of protecting civilians, the medical mission and humanitarian access |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/02/19/situation-tendue-uvira-msf-alerte-sur-lurgence-de-proteger-des-civils-la-mission |access-date=19 February 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> Marching unopposed through the [[Ruzizi Plain]], M23 captured the towns of [[Kamanyola]]<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-02-18 |title=Residents say Rwanda-backed rebels are advancing on a third city in eastern Congo |url=https://apnews.com/article/congo-un-children-killed-bukavu-rebels-0b72da70e3e96b1c100812443fc3201a |access-date=2025-02-19 |website=AP News|first=Wilson|last=McMakin |language=en}}</ref> and [[Sange, Democratic Republic of the Congo|Sange]].<ref name=":58">{{Cite web |date=19 February 2025 |title=RDC: les rebelles de l'AFC/M23 sont arrivés à Sange dans la plaine de la Ruzizi |trans-title=DRC: AFC/M23 rebels have arrived in Sange in the Ruzizi plain |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/02/19/rdc-les-rebelles-de-lafcm23-sont-arrives-sange-dans-la-plaine-de-la-ruzizi |access-date=19 February 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> Residents, fearing potential violence, fled into the mountains.<ref name=":58"/> In response, Burundian forces withdrew from the area back into Burundi.<ref name=":bur5">{{Cite news |date=18 February 2025 |title=Burundi troops withdraw in eastern Congo as M23 rebels expand reach, sources say |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/rebel-held-ports-eastern-dr-congo-re-open-aid-needs-grow-2025-02-18/ |access-date=18 February 2025 |work=Reuters}}</ref> By March 12, M23 had reached to within {{convert|20|mi|km}} from Uvira<ref>{{Cite web |first1=Nick |last1=Markiewicz |first2=Yale |last2=Ford |first3=Liam |last3=Karr |title=Congo War Security Review - March 12, 2025 |date=12 March 2025 |access-date=13 March 2025 |publisher=Critical Threats Project |url=https://www.criticalthreats.org/briefs/congo-war-security-review/congo-war-security-review-march-12-2025 }}</ref> but then withdrew back to Kamanyola after facing international pressure and showing support for a peace conference mediated by [[Angola]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=11 March 2025 |title=Angola will attempt to broker direct talks between Congo and M23, presidency says |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/angola-will-attempt-broker-direct-talks-between-congo-m23-presidency-says-2025-03-11/ |access-date=11 March 2025 |website=[[Reuters]] |publication-place=London, England, United Kingdom |url-access=limited}}</ref> As a ''de facto'' ceasefire set in, M23 took [[Idjwi Territory]] without a fight and now had a presence in seven out of eight of South Kivu's territories.<ref name=":015">{{Cite web |date=14 March 2025 |title=RDC: sur les 8 territoires que compte le Sud-Kivu, 7 sont désormais sous occupation après l'arrivée sans combat des rebelles de l'AFC/M23 au territoire insulaire d'Idjwi |trans-title=DRC: Of the eight territories in South Kivu, seven are now under occupation following the arrival of AFC/M23 rebels in the island territory of Idjwi without a fight |url=https://actualite.cd/index.php/2025/03/14/rdc-sur-les-8-territoires-que-compte-le-sud-kivu-7-sont-desormais-sous-occupation-apres |access-date=14 March 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref>

In South Kivu's [[Fizi Territory]], two ethnic [[Banyamulenge]] militias called [[Twirwaneho]] and [[RED-Tabara]] aligned themselves with M23 and began attacking Congolese positions, taking the village of [[Minembwe]] and its airstrip<ref name= "fizi">{{Cite web |first1=Nick |last1=Markiewicz |first2=Yale |last2=Ford |first3=Liam |last3=Karr |title=Congo War Security Review - February 24, 2025 |date=24 February 2025 |access-date=25 February 2025 |publisher=Critical Threats Project |url=https://www.criticalthreats.org/briefs/congo-war-security-review/congo-war-security-review-february-24-2025 }}</ref> and Mikenge on February 21.<ref name="CTFeb25">{{Cite web |first1=Nick |last1=Markiewicz |first2=Yale |last2=Ford |first3=Liam |last3=Karr |title=Congo War Security Review - February 25, 2025 |date=25 February 2025 |access-date=25 February 2025 |publisher=Critical Threats Project |url=https://www.criticalthreats.org/briefs/congo-war-security-review/congo-war-security-review-february-25-2025#_edn21828e7772430c5f774682fb17670c587 }}</ref> The Congolese military responded by launching an operation to retake them, saying drone strikes killed four Twirwaneho commanders.<ref name= "fizi"/> Fighting quickly spread to neighboring [[Walungu Territory|Walungu]]<ref name="Actualite0103-1">{{Cite web |title=Walungu: FARDC et M23 s'affrontent à Bwahungu et Muzinzi |trans-title=Walungu: FARDC and M23 clash in Bwahungu and Muzinzi |language=fr |date=1 March 2025 |work=[[Actualite.cd]] |access-date=1 March 2025 |url=https://actualite.cd/index.php/2025/03/01/walungu-fardc-et-m23-saffrontent-bwahungu-et-muzinzi}}</ref> and [[Mwenga Territory|Mwenga]] territories.<ref name="Actualite0103-2">{{Cite web |title=Sud-Kivu: violents combats entre Twirwaneho/M23 et Wazalendo à Mwenga |trans-title=South Kivu: heavy fighting between Twirwaneho/M23 and Wazalendo in Mwenga |language=fr |work=[[Actualite.cd]] |date=March 2025 |url=https://actualite.cd/index.php/2025/03/01/sud-kivu-violents-combats-entre-twirwanehom23-et-wazalendo-mwenga }}</ref> After heavy fighting, Congolese soldiers retook Mikenge on March 6<ref>{{Cite web |date=6 March 2025 |title=RDC-Mwenga: les wazalendo reprennent le village Mikenge après de violents combats contre Twirhaneho allié de l'AFC/M23 |trans-title=DRC-Mwenga: Wazalendo retake Mikenge village after violent fighting against Twirhaneho, ally of the AFC/M23 |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/03/06/rdc-mwenga-les-wazalendo-reprennent-le-village-mikenge-apres-de-violents-combats-contre |access-date=6 March 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> while it utilized airstrikes against Twirwaneho positions around Minembwe.<ref>{{Cite web |first1=Nick |last1=Markiewicz |first2=Yale |last2=Ford |first3=Liam |last3=Karr |title=Congo War Security Review - March 10, 2025 |date=10 March 2025 |access-date=10 March 2025 |publisher=Critical Threats Project |url=https://www.criticalthreats.org/briefs/congo-war-security-review/congo-war-security-review-march-10-2025 }}</ref>

==== Walikale front ====
{{main|2025 Walikale offensive}}

On March 6, FARDC and Wazalendo militias attempted a surprise assault to retake Masisi from M23, which had been occupied since 2024.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-03-08 |title=Poursuite des affrontements entre les FARDC et le M23 dans le territoire de Masisi (Nord-Kivu) |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2025/03/08/actualite/securite/poursuite-des-affrontements-entre-les-fardc-et-le-m23-dans-le |access-date=2025-03-20 |website=Radio Okapi |language=fr |archive-date=2025-03-21 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250321211119/https://www.radiookapi.net/2025/03/08/actualite/securite/poursuite-des-affrontements-entre-les-fardc-et-le-m23-dans-le |url-status=live }}</ref> The attack failed and M23 responded by launching a counteroffensive west toward [[Walikale]], the administrative capital of [[Walikale Territory]] in North Kivu. A mining hub rich in raw materials, it has been a contested area throughout the Kivu conflict.<ref name=":FinaTimes">{{Cite web |last=Esther |first=Adela Manema |date=24 August 2024 |title=Nord-Kivu: désenclaver Walikale, pour la sécurité des minerais congolais |trans-title=North Kivu: opening up Walikale for the security of Congolese minerals |url=https://www.financetimes.cd/mines-energies/2024-08-24/nord-kivu-desenclaver-walikale-pour-la-securite-des-minerais-congolais |access-date=20 March 2025 |website=Finance Times |language=fr |archive-date=21 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250321125034/https://www.financetimes.cd/mines-energies/2024-08-24/nord-kivu-desenclaver-walikale-pour-la-securite-des-minerais-congolais |url-status=live }}</ref> Moving from Masisi, M23 quickly marched west along the R529 highway.<ref>{{Cite news |date=March 9, 2025 |title=Clashes continue in eastern Congo days after attack on civilians left many dead |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/clashes-continue-eastern-congo-days-after-attack-civilians-left-many-dead-2025-03-09 |access-date=March 19, 2025 |work=Reuters |archive-date=March 12, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250312190851/https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/clashes-continue-eastern-congo-days-after-attack-civilians-left-many-dead-2025-03-09/ |url-status=live }}</ref> M23 captured the village of Kibati on March 13<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 March 2025 |title=RDC: les rebelles de l'AFC/M23 s'emparent de Kashebere et Kibati en direction de Walikale-centre |trans-title=DRC: AFC/M23 rebels seize Kashebere and Kibati towards Walikale-centre |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/03/13/rdc-les-rebelles-de-lafcm23-semparent-de-kashebere-et-kibati-en-direction-de-walikale |access-date=20 March 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> and then [[Kibua]] three days later after fighting off FARDC and Wazalendo assaults.<ref name=":0162">{{Cite web |date=17 March 2025 |title=RDC: à la veille du début des négociations directes avec Kinshasa, la rébellion de l'AFC/M23 accentue la pression militaire en direction de Walikale-centre, les combats se rapprochent de Mutakato après avoir conquis Mpofi |trans-title=DRC: On the eve of the start of direct negotiations with Kinshasa, the AFC/M23 rebellion increases military pressure towards Walikale-centre, fighting moves closer to Mutakato after conquering Mpofi |url=https://actualite.cd/index.php/2025/03/17/rdc-la-veille-du-debut-des-negociations-directes-avec-kinshasa-la-rebellion-de-lafcm23 |access-date=20 March 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr |archive-date=21 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250321211126/https://actualite.cd/index.php/2025/03/17/rdc-la-veille-du-debut-des-negociations-directes-avec-kinshasa-la-rebellion-de-lafcm23 |url-status=live }}</ref> Receiving reinforcements, FARDC counterattacked, pushing M23 back down the R529.<ref>{{Cite web |date=18 March 2025 |title=RDC: l'armée freine l'avancée de l'AFC/M23 vers Walikale-centre, les rebelles repoussés à Mpofi |trans-title=DRC: Army halts AFC/M23 advance towards Walikale-centre, rebels pushed back to Mpofi |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/03/18/rdc-larmee-freine-lavancee-de-lafcm23-vers-walikale-centre-les-rebelles-repousses-mpofi |access-date=20 March 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> Days of fighting for control of the town of Mpofi and surrounding villages ensued, ending in M23 victory.<ref>{{Cite web |date=18 March 2025 |title=Congo War Security Review |url=https://www.criticalthreats.org/briefs/congo-war-security-review/congo-war-security-review-march-18-2025 |access-date=20 March 2025 |website=Critical Threats |archive-date=21 March 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250321211119/https://www.criticalthreats.org/briefs/congo-war-security-review/congo-war-security-review-march-18-2025 |url-status=live }}</ref> Retreating to Walikale, FARDC set up one last defensive position outside the town but were overwhelmed and dispersed.<ref name="ReuWali">{{Cite news |date=March 19, 2025 |title=M23 rebels enter another eastern Congo town, defying calls for ceasefire |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/m23-rebels-enter-outskirts-east-congo-town-walikale-2025-03-19/ |access-date=March 19, 2025 |work=Reuters}}</ref> M23 captured Walikale on March 19, marking their furthest westward advance.<ref name="ReuWali"/>

During negotiations in [[Qatar]] that same day, Congolese President Tshisekedi and Rwandan President Kagame agreed to a ceasefire.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Mills |first1=Andrew |last2=Ross |first2=Aaron |date=18 March 2025 |title=Exclusive: Congo and Rwanda presidents call for east Congo ceasefire during Qatar sit-down |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/congolese-rwandan-presidents-meet-qatar-discuss-east-congo-conflict-2025-03-18/ |access-date=18 March 2025 |website=[[Reuters]] |publication-place=London, England, United Kingdom |url-access=limited}}</ref> M23 responded by agreeing to withdraw from Walikale, citing it as an effort to "promote conditions for peace initiatives and political dialogue".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rolley |first=Sonia |date=22 March 2025 |title=Congo M23 rebels say they will withdraw from seized town to support peace push |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/congo-m23-rebels-reposition-forces-seized-town-support-peace-efforts-rebel-2025-03-22/ |access-date=22 March 2025 |website=[[Reuters]] |publication-place=London, England, United Kingdom |url-access=limited}}</ref>

