On April 27 in [[Washington, D.C.]], the DRC and Rwanda jointly ratified a declaration of principles aimed at establishing a lasting peace, pledging to mutually recognize each other's sovereignty, address security issues, and promote economic integration, the return of displaced people, support for MONUSCO, and drafting a peace agreement.<ref name=":042">{{Cite web |date=25 April 2025 |title=À Washington, la RDC et le Rwanda s'engagent sur six axes pour ouvrir la voie à un accord de paix |trans-title=In Washington, the DRC and Rwanda commit to six areas to pave the way for a peace agreement |url=https://actualite.cd/index.php/2025/04/25/washington-la-rdc-et-le-rwanda-sengagent-sur-six-axes-pour-ouvrir-la-voie-un-accord-de |access-date=26 April 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref name=":120">{{Cite web |date=25 April 2025 |title=Rwanda and DR Congo set May 2 deadline for peace deal |url=https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20250425-rwanda-dr-congo-set-may-2-deadline-peace-deal-m23-rebels |access-date=26 April 2025 |website=[[France 24]] |language=en |publication-place=Paris, France}}</ref> The document was signed by Congolese Foreign Minister [[Thérèse Kayikwamba Wagner]] and Rwandan Foreign Minister [[Olivier Nduhungirehe]], with [[U.S. Secretary of State]] [[Marco Rubio]] in attendance.<ref name=":042" /><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Knickmeyer |first1=Ellen |last2=Banchereau |first2=Mark |date=25 April 2025 |title=The US oversees a peace pledge for east Congo, hoping to ease its access to critical minerals |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2025/04/25/rubio-congo-rwanda-conflict-minerals-trump/da9611d8-2227-11f0-b141-90a412b995ee_story.html |access-date=26 April 2025 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |language=en-US |issn=0190-8286 |url-access=limited}}</ref> Rubio stressed the importance of these pledges for achieving peace and economic growth in the region, noting that sustainable peace would be conducive to increased foreign investment.<ref>{{Cite web |date=25 April 2025 |title=Congo and Rwanda agree on pathway to peace in US-brokered deal |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/congo-rwanda-agree-pathway-peace-us-brokered-deal-signed-agreement-says-2025-04-25/ |access-date=26 April 2025 |website=[[Reuters]] |publication-place=London, England, United Kingdom}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=25 April 2025 |title=Marco Rubio: "Nos entreprises sont de bons citoyens corporatifs" engagés pour la paix et le développement en Afrique centrale |trans-title=Marco Rubio: "Our companies are good corporate citizens" committed to peace and development in Central Africa |url=https://actualite.cd/index.php/2025/04/25/marco-rubio-nos-entreprises-sont-de-bons-citoyens-corporatifs-engages-pour-la-paix-et-le |access-date=26 April 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> Both parties agreed to submit a preliminary peace agreement by 2 May.<ref name=":120" /><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Ross |first1=Will |last2=Lukiv |first2=Jaroslav |date=26 April 2025 |title=DR Congo and Rwanda vow to agree peace plan by 2 May |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c9w8q91ld75o |access-date=26 April 2025 |website=[[BBC News]] |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=25 April 2025 |title=RDC et Rwanda s'engagent à soumettre un projet d'accord de paix commun d'ici au 2 mai |trans-title=DRC and Rwanda commit to submitting a joint draft peace agreement by May 2 |url=https://actualite.cd/index.php/2025/04/25/rdc-et-rwanda-sengagent-soumettre-un-projet-daccord-de-paix-commun-dici-au-2-mai |access-date=26 April 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref>

Despite the peace deal, M23 continued to fight Wazalendo militias conducting guerilla attacks.<ref>{{Cite web |date=28 April 2025 |title=Sud-Kivu: Kaziba de nouveau occupé par l'AFC/M23 |trans-title=South Kivu: Kaziba occupied again by the AFC/M23 |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/04/28/sud-kivu-kaziba-de-nouveau-occupe-par-lafcm23 |access-date=29 April 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> On 29 April, SADC troops began withdrawing from Goma via the Grande Barrière following an M23 ultimatum demanding their exit from rebel-held areas.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kapinga |first=Yvonne |date=29 April 2025 |title=RDC: Les troupes de la SADC quittent Goma par le Rwanda |trans-title=DRC: SADC troops leave Goma via Rwanda |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/04/29/rdc-les-troupes-de-la-sadc-quittent-goma-par-le-rwanda |access-date=29 April 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> On 27 June, the DRC and Rwanda signed a [[2025 Democratic Republic of the Congo–Rwanda peace agreement|U.S.-mediated peace agreement]] in Washington, attended by Secretary of State [[Marco Rubio]]. Foreign Ministers [[Thérèse Kayikwamba Wagner|Thérèse Wagner]] and [[Olivier Nduhungirehe]] formalized the accord, concluding months of diplomatic negotiations aimed at reducing tensions and conflict in eastern DRC.<ref>{{Cite web |date=27 June 2025 |title=C'est officiel! La RDC et le Rwanda ont signé l'accord de paix |trans-title=It's official! The DRC and Rwanda have signed the peace agreement |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/06/27/cest-officiel-la-rdc-et-le-rwanda-ont-signe-laccord-de-paix |access-date=27 June 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Psaledakis |first1=Daphne |last2=Rolley |first2=Sonia |last3=Kasongo |first3=Ange Adihe |date=27 June 2025 |title=Rwanda, Congo sign peace deal in US to end fighting, attract investment |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/africa/rwanda-democratic-republic-congo-set-sign-peace-agreement-washington-2025-06-27/ |access-date=27 June 2025 |website=[[Reuters]] |publication-place=London, England, United Kingdom}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Princewill |first=Nimi |date=27 June 2025 |title=As two African nations sign a peace deal, Trump wants credit. But some fear peace may still elude them |url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/06/27/africa/trump-drc-rwanda-peace-deal-intl |access-date=27 June 2025 |website=[[CNN]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=27 June 2025 |title=Watch: Trump hosts Congolese, Rwandan officials after US-brokered peace deal signed |url=https://thehill.com/video-clips/5373723-watch-live-donald-trump-marco-rubio-democratic-republic-congo-rwanda-peace-deal/ |access-date=27 June 2025 |website=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |publication-place=Washington, D.C., United States}}</ref> However, the accord did not halt M23's military activities. On 28 June, rebel forces captured the center of [[Kaniola Groupement|Kaniola]] following renewed clashes with Wazalendo.<ref>{{Cite web |date=28 June 2025 |title=RDC: au Sud-Kivu, le M23 s'impose à Kaniola, à 57 km de Bukavu, après des affrontements avec les Wazalendo |trans-title=DRC: In South Kivu, the M23 takes control of Kaniola, 57 km from Bukavu, after clashes with the Wazalendo |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/06/28/rdc-au-sud-kivu-le-m23-simpose-kaniola-57-km-de-bukavu-apres-des-affrontements-avec-les |access-date=29 June 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> Even after signing the Doha Declaration of Principles, which underscored commitments to peaceful dialogue, recognition of Congolese sovereignty, and respect for international law, M23 persisted in carrying out its campaign.<ref name=":662" /><ref name=":0" /><ref name=":76" /> Throughout July, August, and September, M23 operations persisted across North and South Kivu, including forced displacements, civilian killings, and the expansion of rebel control into mining zones and strategic localities.<ref name=":98">{{Cite web |date=20 August 2025 |title=DR Congo: M23 Mass Killings Near Virunga National Park |url=https://www.hrw.org/news/2025/08/20/dr-congo-m23-mass-killings-near-virunga-national-park |access-date=23 August 2025 |website=[[Human Rights Watch]] |language=en |publication-place=New York, New York, United States}}</ref><ref name=":101">{{Cite web |date=22 August 2025 |title=Est de la RDC: l'ONU appelle à une cessation effective des hostilités |trans-title=Eastern DRC: UN calls for effective cessation of hostilities |url=https://www.radiookapi.net/2025/08/22/actualite/politique/est-de-la-rdc-lonu-appelle-une-cessation-effective-des-hostilites |access-date=23 August 2025 |website=[[Radio Okapi]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref name=":108">{{Cite web |date=4 September 2025 |title=Rwanda: forte hausse du nombre de tombes au cimetière militaire de Kigali depuis l'offensive du M23 (HRW) |trans-title=Rwanda: Sharp increase in the number of graves at Kigali military cemetery since the M23 offensive (HRW) |url=https://actualite.cd/index.php/2025/09/04/rwanda-forte-hausse-du-nombre-de-tombes-au-cimetiere-militaire-de-kigali-depuis |access-date=8 September 2025 |website=[[Actualite.cd]] |language=fr}}</ref> On 5 September, the UN accused the "Rwanda-backed rebels" of slaughtering children and other civilians in Congo.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bariyo |first=Nicholas |date=5 September 2025 |title=U.N. Accuses Rwanda-Backed Rebels of Slaughtering Children, Other Civilians in Congo |url=https://www.wsj.com/world/africa/u-n-accuses-rwanda-backed-rebels-of-slaughtering-children-other-civilians-in-congo-0d073edb |access-date=25 September 2025 |website=[[The Wall Street Journal]]}}</ref>

The front lines largely remained frozen from March to December 2025.<ref name="TRT-09122025">{{Cite web |title=M23 rebels enter key city in eastern DRC, forcing thousands to flee |work=[[TRT World]] |date=9 December 2025 |access-date=9 December 2025 |url=https://www.trtworld.com/article/1bcc51afdcc2 }}</ref> M23 may have quadrupled its active troops since early 2025, with an estimate in October putting its force at 22,000 fighters, making it the largest DRC rebellion since the Second Congo War.<ref>{{Cite web |first1=Michael |last1=DeAngelo |first2=Miles |last2=Charles |first3=Yale |last3=Ford |first4=Liam |last4=Karr |title=Mali Negotiates Amid JNIM Blockade; M23 Numbers Swell: Africa File, October 9, 2025 |date=October 9, 2025 |access-date=November 3, 2025 |work=Critical Threats Project |publisher=[[Institute for the Study of War]] |url=https://www.criticalthreats.org/analysis/mali-negotiates-jnim-blockade-drc-m23-numbers-swell-al-shabaab-mogadishu-somali-election-disputes-boko-haram-cameroon-nigeria-border-africa-file-october-9-2025 }}</ref> Burundi, which sees the advance of Rwandan-backed rebels in South Kivu as an existential threat due to the region's proximity to its economic center [[Bujumbura]], deployed additional troops in May and started building up defenses in November.<ref name="TRT-09122025" /> M23 militarized the areas south of Bukavu along National Road 5 with additional troops and equipment in late November.<ref name="auto">{{Cite web |last=Ford |first=Yale |title=Congo War Security Review, December 3, 2025 |access-date=December 4, 2025 |work=Critical Threats Project |publisher=Institute for the Study of War |date=December 3, 2025 |url=https://www.criticalthreats.org/briefs/congo-war-security-review/december-3-2025 }}</ref> Burundi's foreign minister claimed that Rwanda sent "trucks full of soldiers" from 28 November to reinforce its 8,000 troops in South Kivu.<ref name="SOSBurundi1">{{Cite web |title=South Kivu : M23 seizes Sange, triggering massive influx into Burundi and a retreat of Burundian troops |date=9 December 2025 |access-date=9 December 2025 |work=SOS Médias Burundi |url=https://www.sosmediasburundi.org/en/2025/12/09/south-kivu-m23-seizes-sange-triggering-a-massive-influx-into-burundi-and-a-retreat-of-burundian-troops/ }}</ref>

=== Renewed conflict ===
==== Second Uvira offensive ====
On December 1, M23 relaunched its offensive toward Uvira from its forward position at Kamanyola.<ref name="TRT-09122025" /> The strategic town of [[Lubarika]] fell to the group the next day, and separate attacks were launched towards [[Luvungi]], a key FARDC stronghold. Congolese soldiers, assisted by Burundian forces, counterattacked on the same day, pushing M23 back from the town.<ref name="auto"/><ref>{{Cite web |title=DRC Conflict: Hundreds of Burundian soldiers cross to South Kivu as clashes intensify |date=6 December 2025 |access-date=10 December 2025 |url=https://thegreatlakeseye.com/post?s=DRC--Conflict%3A--Hundreds--of--Burundian--soldiers--cross--to--South--Kivu--as--clashes--intensify_2040 }}</ref> M23 brought reinforcements from Bukavu and the Lake Kivu area and attacked again, breaking through FARDC defenses to take Luvungi on December 4.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Ford |first1=Yale |last2=Zhu |first2=Anping |title=Congo War Security Review, December 5, 2025 |access-date=December 9, 2025 |work=Critical Threats Project |publisher=Institute for the Study of War |date=December 5, 2025 |url=https://www.criticalthreats.org/briefs/congo-war-security-review/congo-war-security-review-december-5-2025 }}</ref> FARDC troops retreated south to [[Luberizi]] as M23 advanced toward Uvira,<ref name="ISW-08122025">{{Cite web |last=Ford |first=Yale |title=Congo War Security Review, December 8, 2025 |access-date=December 9, 2025 |work=Critical Threats Project |publisher=Institute for the Study of War |date=December 8, 2025  |url=https://www.criticalthreats.org/briefs/congo-war-security-review/congo-war-security-review-december-8-2025 }}</ref> which was made the temporary capital of South Kivu after the fall of Bukavu,<ref name="Actualite-09122025">{{Cite web |last=Muamba |first=Clement |title=Uvira: face à la progression de l'AFC/M23, plus de 20 000 personnes ont déjà trouvé refuge à Bujumbura en une journée |trans-title=Uvira: Faced with the progress of the AFC/M23, more than 20 000 people have already found refuge in Bujumbura in one day |language=fr |work=[[Actualite.cd]] |date=9 December 2025 |access-date=9 December 2025 |url=https://actualite.cd/index.php/2025/12/09/uvira-face-la-progression-de-lafcm23-plus-de-20-000-personnes-ont-deja-trouve-refuge }}</ref> and the province's last major settlement outside of rebel control.<ref name="TRT-09122025" /> M23 expanded its control of surrounding areas as Congolese and Burundian troops retreated amidst reports of increasing casualties, including the latter's commander.<ref name="ISW-08122025" /> Infighting among FARDC forces and Wazalendo militias in the town of Sange triggered a collapse in government defenses and it was captured by M23 on December 8.<ref name="ISW-08122025" /> As a disorderly withdrawal took place, Burundi ordered all its troops to return to the country and defend its border.<ref name="SOSBurundi1"/> FARDC, Wazalendo, and Burundian troops began fleeing "en masse" from Uvira.<ref name="ISW-08122025" /> M23 fighters entered the city on late 9 December<ref name="TRT-09122025" /> and announced it under their total control on the next day.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Rwanda-backed M23 rebels say they've seized the strategic city of Uvira in eastern Congo |date=10 December 2025 |access-date=10 December 2025 |work=ABC News |url=https://abcnews.go.com/Business/wireStory/rwanda-backed-m23-rebels-seized-strategic-city-uvira-128273234 }}</ref> Regional authorities and the United Nations said at least 400 people were killed and 200,000 more were displaced in the fighting.<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 December 2025 |title=Residents emerge in DR Congo's tense Uvira after M23 rebel takeover |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/12/13/residents-emerge-in-dr-congos-tense-uvira-after-m23-rebel-takeover |access-date=16 December 2025 |website=Al Jazeera}}</ref>

M23 then continued south from Uvira, taking areas around the [[Itombwe Mountains]] and clashing with Congolese militias on the plateaus overlooking Fizi Territory.<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 December 2025 |title=M23 marches on in east DR Congo as US vows action against Rwanda |url=https://www.rfi.fr/en/international-news/20251213-m23-marches-on-in-east-dr-congo-as-us-vows-action-against-rwanda |access-date=13 December 2025 |website=RFI}}</ref> Making their way down the western shore of [[Lake Tanganyika]], M23 
took the town of Makobola, site of the infamous [[Makobola massacre|1998–99 massacre]], and surrounding villages.<ref>{{Cite web |date=15 December 2025 |title=In South Kivu, the town of Makobola and the village of Kasekezi have fallen under the control of the AFC/M23 rebels |url=https://actualite.cd/2025/12/15/au-sud-kivu-la-cite-de-makobola-et-le-village-kasekezi-passent-sous-controle-des |access-date=16 December 2025 |website=Actualite.cd |language=fr}}</ref>

== Analysis ==
=== Rwandan role ===
{{Main|Democratic Republic of the Congo–Rwanda conflict}}

{{Further|Democratic Republic of the Congo–Rwanda relations}}
As a result of the M23 advances in 2022, [[Bintou Keita]], top UN official for the DRC, described the group as having "conducted itself as a conventional army, rather than an armed group," and warned that the group's capabilities exceeded that of MONUSCO.<ref>{{Citation |title=UN: Well-Armed M23 Rebels Resurgent in DRC |last=Besheer |first=Margaret |date=2 July 2022 |url=https://www.voanews.com/a/un-well-armed-m23-rebels-resurgent-in-drc/6638775.html |publication-date=29 June 2022 |publisher=[[Voice of America]] |access-date=9 August 2022 |archive-date=7 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220807103532/https://www.voanews.com/a/un-well-armed-m23-rebels-resurgent-in-drc/6638775.html |url-status=live}}</ref> According to [[United Nations Security Council]] researchers, the presence of individuals in Rwandan uniforms among the rebels has been proven through photos and [[Unmanned aerial vehicle|drone]] footage, partially explaining the M23 forces' increased professionalism.<ref name="RadioOkapi3"/> Congolese researcher Josaphat Musamba concurred, arguing that it was "clear that there is support" behind M23's resurgence. ''Congo Research Group'' director [[Jason Stearns]] stated that, though there was "no certainty" about Rwanda backing the M23 offensive, the rebels' firepower and various frontline reports made Rwandan involvement "very likely". Regardless of Rwanda's possible role in the offensive, analysts cautioned that M23 had never been just a Rwandan pawn, and always maintained its own agenda.<ref name="Sengenya Huon">{{cite web |author1=Claude Sengenya |author2=Patricia Huon |date=7 July 2022 |title=Revived M23 rebellion worsens DR Congo's security troubles |url=https://www.thenewhumanitarian.org/analysis/2022/07/07/Congo-M23-Rwanda-martial-law-demobilisation-ADF-CODECO |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220707180734/https://www.thenewhumanitarian.org/analysis/2022/07/07/Congo-M23-Rwanda-martial-law-demobilisation-ADF-CODECO |archive-date=7 July 2022 |access-date=7 July 2022 |work=[[The New Humanitarian]]}}</ref>

In early August 2022, a report for the UN by independent researchers provided further evidence about Rwandan support for M23, including photos and videos showcasing Rwandan soldiers moving into Congolese territory and M23 troops armed with Rwandan weaponry.<ref name="RadioOkapi4"/><ref>{{cite web |url= https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/08/05/actualite/politique/rdc-le-gouvernement-salue-le-rapport-de-lonu-sur-le-soutien-du-rwanda |title=RDC: le gouvernement salue le rapport de l'ONU sur le soutien du Rwanda au M23 |work=Radio Okapi |date=5 August 2022 |access-date=19 August 2022 |archive-date=26 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230226042221/https://www.radiookapi.net/2022/08/05/actualite/politique/rdc-le-gouvernement-salue-le-rapport-de-lonu-sur-le-soutien-du-rwanda |url-status=live}}</ref> In October, a Rwandan soldier surrendered to MONUSCO at Kiwanja; the Congolese government regarded this as a further proof of Rwandan support for the rebel offensive.<ref name="Etahoben1"/> By January 2023, the United States, several European countries, and UN experts believed that Rwanda was supporting M23.<ref name="DefensePost"/>

By 2025 it was estimated that 4000 to 7000 RDF soldiers were fighting in Congo and had suffered significant casualties. Satellite images also showed significant expansion in the Kanombe military cemetery in Kigali, where at least 600 graves have been dug since the beginning of the offensive.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Townsend |first1=Mark |last2=Wrong |first2=Michela |title=Rwandan troops 'dying in large numbers in DRC', despite official denials of role |url= https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2025/feb/07/democratic-republic-congo-drc-paul-kagame-m23-rebels-goma-rwanda-troops-dying-denials |access-date=12 February 2025 |work=The Guardian |date=7 February 2025}}</ref>

==== Economic and commercial interests ====
{{Multiple image
| total_width       = 410
| image1            = Bunagana.jpg
| image2            = M23 troops Bunagana 4.jpg
| footer            = M23 troops in the mining town of [[Bunagana, Democratic Republic of the Congo|Bunagana]]
| footer_align      = center
}}

Critics argue that both M23 and Rwanda have opportunistically exploited the presence of the FDLR as a pretext for broader political and economic ambitions in eastern DRC,<ref name=":510">{{cite web |last=Gras |first=Romain |date=6 January 2025 |title=RDC: que reste-t-il des FDLR dans l'Est? |trans-title=DRC: What remains of the FDLR in the East? |url=https://www.jeuneafrique.com/1639204/politique/rdc-que-reste-t-il-des-fdlr-dans-lest/ |access-date=28 February 2025 |website=[[Jeune Afrique]] |language=fr-FR}}</ref><ref name="Lagarde2025" /><ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Wrong |first=Michela |date=3 March 2025 |title=How Far Will Rwanda Go in Congo? |url=https://www.foreignaffairs.com/democratic-republic-congo/how-far-will-rwanda-go-congo |access-date=5 March 2025 |website=[[Foreign Affairs]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=17 January 2014 |title="Il faut renforcer la lutte contre les FDLR" |trans-title="We must strengthen the fight against the FDLR" |url=https://www.bbc.com/afrique/region/2014/01/140117_rdcongo_rdc_fdlr_monusco |access-date=5 March 2025 |website=BBC News Afrique |language=fr}}</ref> particularly since the FDLR no longer poses a substantial military threat to Rwanda.<ref name="Bashi2022" /><ref name="StearnsAug2022" /><ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=14 February 2025 |title=Rwanda et FDLR: manipulation pour tuer et piller la RDC |trans-title=Rwanda and FDLR: Manipulation to Kill and Plunder the DRC |url=https://heshimardc.net/v1/2025/02/14/rwanda-et-fdlr-manipulation-pour-tuer-et-piller-la-rdc/ |access-date=5 March 2025 |website=Heshima Magazine |language=fr-FR}}</ref><ref name="Fabricius2024" /> A December 2023 UNSC-commissioned report revealed that since October 2023, RDF soldiers had been deployed in [[Nyiragongo Territory|Nyiragongo]], [[Rutshuru Territory|Rutshuru]], and [[Masisi Territory|Masisi]] territories, with RDF and M23 receiving support from 250 former FDLR combatants under Rwanda's Defence Intelligence Directorate.<ref>{{Cite web |date=30 December 2023 |title=Midterm report of the Group of Experts on the Democratic Republic of the Congo |url=https://docs.un.org/en/s/2023/990 |access-date=28 February 2025 |website=Docs.un.org/en |publisher=[[United Nations Security Council]] |page=10}}</ref> There is substantial evidence suggesting that rebel factions backed by Rwanda and Uganda, including M23, control strategic yet informal supply chains that transport valuable minerals from the [[Kivu]] region to both countries.<ref name=":74">{{Cite web |date=17 January 2013 |title=M23 Rebels Making Millions through Gold Smuggling from Eastern Congo – Democratic Republic of the Congo |url=https://reliefweb.int/report/democratic-republic-congo/m23-rebels-making-millions-through-gold-smuggling-eastern-congo |access-date=28 February 2025 |website=reliefweb.int |publisher=[[ReliefWeb]] |language=en}}</ref> The corridor from [[Bunagana, Democratic Republic of the Congo|Bunagana]] on the Ugandan border, through [[Kanyabayonga]] to Goma on the Rwandan border, encompasses a lucrative mining belt containing some of the world's largest deposits of [[coltan]], a mineral essential for the production of electronic devices.<ref name=":74" /><ref name=":622" /> The DRC is also the world's largest producer of [[cobalt]], a key component in [[Electric vehicle battery|electric vehicle batteries]], which are in high demand globally.<ref>{{Cite web |first=Dorothée |last=Baumann-Pauly |date=29 October 2020 |title=Why Cobalt Mining in the DRC Needs Urgent Attention |url=https://www.cfr.org/blog/why-cobalt-mining-drc-needs-urgent-attention |access-date=13 May 2025 |work=[[Council on Foreign Relations]] |language=en}}</ref>

In 2021, U.S. data indicated that Rwanda accounted for 15% of the world's [[tantalum]] supply, a derivative of coltan ore, despite producing only modest amounts domestically.<ref name=":611">{{Cite web |date=April 2024 |title=Blood minerals: The laundering of DRC's 3T minerals by Rwanda and private entities |url=https://amsterdamandpartners.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/2024.04.25-AP-DRC-Blood-Minerals.pdf |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240830095520/https://amsterdamandpartners.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/2024.04.25-AP-DRC-Blood-Minerals.pdf |url-status=dead |archive-date=2024-08-30 |access-date=22 February 2025 |website=Amsterdamandpartners.com |publisher=Amsterdam & Partners LLP |pages=5–6 |publication-place=Washington DC, United States |ref={{harvid|Amsterdam & Partners LLP|2024}}}}</ref> In March 2023, Congolese Finance Minister [[Nicolas Kazadi]] reported that the country was losing approximately [[Dollar sign|$]]1 billion annually due to minerals being illicitly smuggled into Rwanda. He noted that Rwanda's 2022 mineral exports, including [[gold]], [[tin]], tantalum, and [[tungsten]], were valued at nearly $1 billion, despite the country having limited known reserves.<ref name=":611" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Amunazo |first=Pamela |date=12 September 2023 |title=Exploitation minière: la RDC a perdu 1 milliard de dollars américain l'année dernière |trans-title=DRC has lost US$1 billion in potential mining revenue |url=https://www.bbc.com/afrique/articles/c8v5l2jn0qeo |access-date=8 March 2025 |website=[[BBC News]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kabore |first=Elie |date=12 September 2023 |title=RDC: la RDC a perdu plus de 600 milliards de FCFA de son revenu potentiel de l'exploitation minière {{!}} Mines Actu Burkina |trans-title=DRC: DRC has lost more than 600 billion FCFA of its potential income from mining |url=https://minesactu.info/2023/09/12/rdc-la-rdc-a-perdu-plus-de-600-milliards-de-fcfa-de-son-revenu-potentiel-de-lexploitation-miniere/ |access-date=8 March 2025 |website=Mines Actu Burkina |language=fr-FR |publication-place=Ouagadougou, Burkina Faso}}</ref> The U.S. also purchased 36% of its tantalum imports from Rwanda, more than from any other country, while only 7% came from the DRC, a major tantalum producer. Rwanda has utilized an international network of elites to facilitate the smuggling, sale, and monetization of DRC minerals through militarized trade routes.<ref name=":611" /> Kigali has received institutional backing to commercialize these minerals via an industry-led compliance framework and companies such as [[AVX Corporation]], [[KEMET Corporation]], and [[Talison Minerals|Global Advanced Metals]], which manufacture electronic components. These firms have been accused of knowingly purchasing minerals of illicit origin.<ref name=":611" /> Due diligence teams from major technology companies, including [[Apple Inc.|Apple]], [[Intel]], [[Sony]], [[Motorola]], and [[Lockheed Martin]], have also been informed that minerals sourced from Rwanda were likely smuggled from the DRC, often under conditions of violent exploitation.<ref name=":611" /> Despite this, these minerals continue to be integrated into global supply chains for products ranging from laptops to aircraft. Rwanda, in turn, has cultivated an image as a stable and efficient trading hub within the mineral sector.<ref name=":611" />

[[File: SRSG visits coltan mine in Rubaya (13406579753).jpg|thumb|[[Rubaya mines|Bibatama Mining Concession]], the largest [[coltan]] mine in the [[African Great Lakes]] region and a major contributor to the country's mining economy, accounting for 15% of global coltan production, has been under M23 control since April 2024.<ref>{{Cite web |date=30 January 2025 |title=RDC: le M23 contrôle déjà l'exploitation d'une grande partie du coltan du Nord-Kivu |trans-title=DRC: M23 already controls the exploitation of a large part of coltan in North Kivu |url=https://www.rfi.fr/fr/%C3%A9conomie/20250130-rdc-le-m23-contr%C3%B4le-d%C3%A9j%C3%A0-l-exploitation-d-une-grande-partie-du-coltan-du-nord-kivu |access-date=1 March 2025 |website=[[Radio France Internationale]] |language=fr}}</ref>]]

On 30 April 2024, M23 and RDF forces seized key mining areas in southern [[Masisi Territory]], including [[Rubaya mines|Rubaya]], one of the world's largest coltan deposits. The group consolidated control over commercial centers in Rubaya and Mushaki and secured key transport routes used to move minerals into Rwanda. This control produced one of the largest recorded infiltrations of ineligible [[Conflict minerals law|3T minerals]] into regional supply chains in the [[African Great Lakes]] area in the past decade.<ref name=":112">{{Cite web |date=27 December 2024 |title=United Nations Group of Experts on the Democratic Republic of the Congo |url=https://docs.un.org/en/s/2024/969 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250404130414/https://docs.un.org/en/s/2024/969 |archive-date=4 April 2025 |access-date=15 December 2025 |website=[[United Nations Security Council]] |pages=12–15 |publication-place=New York, New York, United States}}</ref> M23 established a monopoly over coltan exports from Rubaya to Rwanda as well as prioritized large-scale shipments, and imposed extensive taxation. The group managed every stage of mineral extraction, trade, and transport, and effectively functioned as a parallel governing authority. In Rubaya's commercial zone, it set up a so-called ministry of mineral exploitation that issued permits to miners and traders bearing labels of the "République Démocratique du Congo – Province du Nord Kivu". Annual fees were imposed on miners and traders, while miner wages were doubled to secure continued production.<ref name=":112" /> M23 strictly enforced transaction rules and threatened sanctions for violations. Coltan from Rubaya was transported along multiple routes within the DRC, including Bihambwe, Mushaki, Kirolirwe, [[Kitchanga|Kitshanga]], Kizimba, [[Bwito Chiefdom#Groupements|Bishusha]], Mulimbi, [[Bwito Chiefdom#Groupements|Tongo]], Kalengera, and [[Bukumu Chiefdom#Groupements|Kibumba]]. At the Kibumba market in [[Bwito Chiefdom]], loads were frequently transferred to heavy trucks entering from Rwanda, a movement corroborated by [[satellite imagery]].<ref name=":112" /> The group also imposed forced labor (''salongo'') on local populations to build and widen roads used for mineral transport. Reports indicated that convoys operated routinely, shipping several tons of coltan each week, approximately 120 tons per month, while AFC-M23 collected taxes and in-kind payments that generated an estimated $800,000 monthly from Rubaya's coltan trade.<ref name=":112" /> Once exported via Rwanda, smuggled coltan entered global supply chains through international traders and refiners. Customs records reviewed by [[Global Witness]] show that Traxys, a [[Luxembourg]]-based multinational commodities firm, purchased 280 tonnes of coltan from Rwanda and was nearly the sole buyer from Rwandan exporter African Panther Resources Limited.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=15 April 2025 |title=New investigation suggests EU trader Traxys buys conflict minerals from DRC |url=https://globalwitness.org/en/campaigns/transition-minerals/new-investigation-suggests-eu-trader-traxys-buys-conflict-minerals-from-drc/ |access-date=15 December 2025 |website=[[Global Witness]] |language=en}}</ref> African Panther's coltan exports surged to unprecedented levels in 2024, surpassing the combined total recorded over the previous four years. This spike coincided with the intensification of the conflict in North Kivu and increased smuggling from Rubaya, which indicates that a substantial share of African Panther's 2024 exports likely originated from conflict zones in the DRC.<ref name=":1" /> Traxys significantly increased its purchases of Rwandan coltan in 2023 and became one of Rwanda's largest buyers in 2024, despite clear evidence by late 2023 that conflict coltan from Masisi was being systematically smuggled and laundered into supply chains.<ref name=":1" /> The laundering of smuggled minerals is facilitated by weaknesses in traceability and certification systems, as multiple investigations have shown that even industry-accepted schemes have at times been used to legitimize minerals of illicit origin. Methods such as blending coltan from different sources or altering its physical appearance have been documented as techniques to obscure provenance.<ref name=":1" />

In a separate but related case, the [[International Court of Justice]] (ICJ) ruled in February 2022 that Uganda must pay $325 million to the DRC in reparations for its role in conflicts between 1998 and 2003, which included the deaths of thousands of civilians in [[Ituri Province]], the funding of rebel groups, and the illicit extraction of natural resources such as gold, [[diamond]]s, and [[Lumber|timber]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=10 February 2022 |title=ICJ orders Uganda to pay $325m for DR Congo occupation |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-africa-60323990 |access-date=28 February 2025 |work=[[BBC News]] |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref name=":622" /> Since then, gold has become Uganda's leading export, with much of it reportedly originating from DRC mines.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Neiman |first=Sophie |date=8 October 2021 |title=Uganda's Illegal Gold Market Is Bustling |url=https://www.worldpoliticsreview.com/uganda-s-illegal-gold-market-is-bustling/ |access-date=28 February 2025 |website=World Politics Review}}</ref> Similarly, while the DRC accounted for 40% of the world's coltan output in 2019,<ref name=":622" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ojewale |first=Oluwole |date=13 May 2022 |title=Addressing the enablers of coltan smuggling in the DRC requires holistic solutions |url=https://www.theafricareport.com/203796/addressing-the-enablers-of-coltan-smuggling-in-the-drc-requires-holistic-solutions/ |access-date=28 February 2025 |website=[[The Africa Report]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=21 January 2021 |title=Why it's hard for Congo's coltan miners to abide by the law |url=https://www.economist.com/middle-east-and-africa/2021/01/21/why-its-hard-for-congos-coltan-miners-to-abide-by-the-law |access-date=28 February 2025 |newspaper=[[The Economist]] |issn=0013-0613}}</ref> substantial amounts are reportedly funneled illicitly into Rwanda, Uganda, and Burundi.<ref>{{Cite web |date=3 May 2001 |title=Security Council condemns illegal exploitation of Democratic Republic of Congo's natural resources |url=https://press.un.org/en/2001/sc7057.doc.htm |access-date=28 February 2025 |website=press.un.org |publisher=[[United Nations Security Council]]}}</ref> UN investigations have found that while most of the DRC's [[contraband]] coltan is routed through Rwanda, a notable portion is also transported via [[Bunagana, Democratic Republic of the Congo|Bunagana]] and [[Rutshuru]] into Uganda and through [[Uvira]] into Burundi.<ref name=":622" />

==== European Union agreements and controversy ====
In October 2023, the [[European Union]] (EU) signed a partnership with the DRC and Zambia to develop raw material supply chains, including infrastructure investment and sustainable resource extraction. The EU stated that securing a stable supply of critical minerals was essential for meeting its green energy and climate goals.<ref>{{Cite web |date=25 October 2023 |title=Global Gateway: EU signs strategic partnerships on critical raw materials value chains with DRC and Zambia and advances cooperation with US and other key partners to develop the 'Lobito Corridor' |url=https://ec.europa.eu/commission/presscorner/detail/en/ip_23_5303 |access-date=5 March 2025 |website=[[European Commission]]}}</ref><ref name=":612">{{harvnb|Amsterdam & Partners LLP|2024|p=8}}</ref> In February 2024, the EU announced a similar agreement with Rwanda, aiming to "nurture sustainable and resilient value chains for critical raw materials".<ref>{{Cite web |date=February 2024 |title=EU and Rwanda sign a Memorandum of Understanding on Sustainable Raw Materials Value Chains |url=https://ec.europa.eu/commission/presscorner/detail/ne/ip_24_822 |access-date=5 March 2025 |website=[[European Commission]]}}</ref> The EU's investments in Rwanda's mineral sector between 2021 and 2024 amounted to $260 million. The agreement recognized Rwanda as a key player in global tantalum production, as well as a producer of tin, tungsten, gold, [[niobium]], and potentially [[lithium]] and rare earth elements.<ref name=":612" /> The EU further praised Rwanda's regulatory environment, stating that it could serve as a hub for mineral processing and value addition. The announcement was met with immediate criticism from the [[Government of the Democratic Republic of the Congo|Congolese government]] and civil society organizations. President [[Félix Tshisekedi]] described the deal as a "provocation of very, very bad taste", arguing that it would legitimize mineral smuggling and exploitation.<ref name=":612" /><ref name=":4">{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=23 February 2024 |title=Accord UE-Rwanda sur les minerais critiques: "C'est une provocation de très mauvais goût" Félix Tshisekedi |trans-title=EU-Rwanda agreement on critical minerals: "It's a provocation in very bad taste" Félix Tshisekedi |url=https://miningandbusiness.com/2024/02/23/accord-ue-rwanda-sur-les-minerais-critiques-cest-une-provocation-de-tres-mauvais-gout-felix-tshisekedi/ |access-date=6 March 2025 |website=Mining and Business |language=fr-FR}}</ref><ref name=":62">{{Cite web |last=Lukoki |first=Eric |date=February 2024 |title=Accord UE-Rwanda sur les matières premières, "c'est vraiment une provocation de très mauvais goût" (Tshisekedi) |trans-title=EU-Rwanda agreement on raw materials, "it is really a provocation in very bad taste" (Tshisekedi) |url=https://topcongo.live/articles/accord-ue-rwanda-sur-les-matieres-premieres-cest-vraiment-une-provocation-de-tres-mauvais-gout-tshisekedi |access-date=5 March 2025 |website=Topcongo.live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Dossou |first=Modeste |date=24 February 2024 |title=L'Union européenne aide le Rwanda à piller les ressources de la RDC (Félix Tshisekedi) |trans-title=European Union helps Rwanda plunder DRC resources (Felix Tshisekedi) |url=https://actucameroun.com/2024/02/24/lunion-europeenne-aide-le-rwanda-a-piller-les-ressources-de-la-rdc-felix-tshisekedi/ |access-date=5 March 2025 |website=Actu Cameroun |language=fr-FR}}</ref> He asserted that the agreement effectively allowed Rwanda to profit from "the blood of our compatriots".<ref name=":4" /><ref name=":62" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Bakumba |first=Odon |date=23 February 2024 |title=Accord UE-Rwanda: Félix Tshisekedi dénonce une "provocation de mauvais goût" et annonce des actions judiciaires |trans-title=EU-Rwanda Agreement: Félix Tshisekedi denounces a "provocation in bad taste" and announces legal action |url=https://beto.cd/actualite/encontinu/2024/02/23/accord-ue-rwanda-felix-tshisekedi-denonce-une-provocation-de-mauvais-gout-et-annonce-des-actions-judiciaires.html/156417/ |access-date=5 March 2025 |website=BETO.CD |language=fr-FR}}</ref> DRC Foreign Minister [[Christophe Lutundula]] accused the EU of complicity in the "looting and aggression" against the DRC, citing other EU-Rwanda agreements on security and military cooperation.<ref name=":612" /><ref>{{Cite news |last=Châtelot |first=Christophe |date=27 February 2024 |title=RDC: "L'Union européenne se rend complice du pillage de nos ressources et de l'agression du Rwanda" |trans-title=DRC: "The European Union is complicit in the plundering of our resources and the aggression against Rwanda" |url=https://www.lemonde.fr/afrique/article/2024/02/27/rdc-l-union-europeenne-se-rend-complice-du-pillage-de-nos-ressources-et-de-l-agression-du-rwanda_6218905_3212.html |access-date=5 March 2025 |work=[[Le Monde]] |language=fr}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Châtelot |first=Christophe |date=28 February 2024 |title="L'Union européenne se rend complice du pillage de nos ressources et de l'agression du Rwanda" (Christophe Lutundula) |trans-title="The European Union is complicit in the plundering of our resources and the aggression against Rwanda" (Christophe Lutundula) |url=https://www.mediacongo.net/article-actualite-134065_l_union_europeenne_se_rend_complice_du_pillage_de_nos_ressources_et_de_l_agression_du_rwanda_christophe_lutundula.html |access-date=5 March 2025 |website=Mediacongo.net |language=fr |publication-place=Kinshasa, Democratic Republic of the Congo}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Jacqué |first=Philippe |date=25 June 2024 |title=Malgré les pressions congolaises, l'UE discute d'une poursuite de son soutien à l'armée rwandaise au Mozambique |trans-title=Despite Congolese pressure, EU discusses continued support for Rwandan army in Mozambique |url=https://www.lemonde.fr/afrique/article/2024/06/25/malgre-les-pressions-congolaises-l-ue-discute-d-une-poursuite-de-son-soutien-a-l-armee-rwandaise-au-mozambique_6243575_3212.html |access-date=6 March 2025 |work=[[Le Monde]] |language=fr}}</ref> Notably, Rwandan President [[Paul Kagame]] himself acknowledged that Rwanda serves as a transit point for smuggled Congolese minerals. He stated that minerals from the DRC pass through Rwanda to destinations including [[Brussels]], [[Tel Aviv]], [[Russia]], and [[Dubai]].<ref name=":612" />

In February 2024, the EU and Rwanda signed a [[Memorandum of understanding|Memorandum of Understanding]] on Sustainable Raw Materials Value Chains, in which they committed to closer cooperation on integrating and diversifying raw-material value chains, promoting responsible and traceable production aligned with [[Environmental, social, and governance|Environmental, Social and Governance]] (ESG) standards, combating illegal trafficking, mobilizing infrastructure financing, advancing research and innovation, building regulatory and technical capacity, and leveraging the EU's Global Gateway to support investment, skills development, transparency, and structural economic transformation in Rwanda.<ref>{{Cite web |date=18 February 2024 |title=EU and Rwanda sign a Memorandum of Understanding on Sustainable Raw Materials Value Chains |url=https://ec.europa.eu/commission/presscorner/detail/en/https:////ec.europa.eu//commission//presscorner//detail//en//ip_24_822 |access-date=15 December 2025 |website=Ec.europa.eu |publisher=[[European Commission]]}}</ref><ref name=":1" /> In February 2025, the [[European Parliament]] criticized the EU's insufficient response to the crisis in eastern DRC, while EU foreign ministers declined to impose immediate sanctions on Rwanda over its alleged involvement in the conflict. EU foreign policy chief [[Kaja Kallas]] stated that Rwanda had been urged to withdraw its forces and that the raw-materials memorandum would be reviewed.<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Jones |first=Mared Gwyn |date=24 February 2025 |title=EU to review raw materials deal with Rwanda over DRC conflict - top diplomat |url=https://www.euronews.com/my-europe/2025/02/24/eu-to-review-raw-materials-deal-with-rwanda-over-drc-conflict-top-diplomat |access-date=15 December 2025 |website=Euronews.com |publisher=[[Euronews]] |publication-place=Lyon, France}}</ref> On 17 March 2025, the [[European Commission]] imposed restrictive measures on nine individuals and one entity for serious human rights violations and exploitation of the conflict in the DRC, including M23's president [[Bertrand Bisimwa]], recruitment and propaganda chief Désiré Rukomera, Col. John Imani Nzenze, finance deputy Jean-Bosco Nzabonimpa Mupenzi, and North Kivu's M23-appointed governor Joseph Musanga Bahati, as well as on [[Rwandan Defence Force|RDF]] commanders Ruki Karusisi, Major General Eugene Nkubito, and brigadier general Pascal Muhizi for sustaining the conflict, and additionally sanctioned Rwanda Mines, Petroleum and Gas Board (RMB) CEO Francis Kamanzi for illicit conflict-mineral exploitation and the Kigali-based Gasabo Gold Refinery for illegally importing gold from M23-controlled areas of the DRC.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |date=17 March 2025 |title=Democratic Republic of the Congo: EU lists further nine individuals and one entity |url=https://www.consilium.europa.eu/en/press/press-releases/2025/03/17/democratic-republic-of-the-congo-eu-lists-further-nine-individuals-and-one-entity/ |access-date=15 December 2025 |website=Consilium.europa.eu/en |publisher=[[European Union]] |language=en}}</ref> It brought together the total EU sanctions related to the DRC to 32 individuals and two entities subject to travel bans, asset freezes, and a prohibition on EU citizens and companies providing them with funds.<ref name=":2" /> Luxembourg, where Traxys is headquartered, has reportedly blocked similar sanctions in the past. Despite these actions, a Global Witness investigation concluded that the EU had not established sufficient safeguards to prevent conflict minerals from entering its market.<ref name=":1" /> As major donors, the EU and its member states retain significant leverage over Rwanda, and adherence to EU values would necessitate suspending development aid until Rwanda withdraws from the DRC and ceases all backing of M23.<ref name=":1" />

==== Geopolitical and economic rivalry between Rwanda and Uganda ====
In November 2020, the Ugandan construction company Dott Services, a co-financier and developer of road networks linking Uganda and the DRC, entered a joint venture with the Congolese state-owned mining company [[Société Aurifère du Kivu et du Maniema]] (Sakima).<ref name=":622" /> This agreement granted Dott Services access to strategic mining sites in [[Maniema|Maniema Province]], an area rich in tin, tantalum, gold, and tungsten. Under the terms of the deal, Dott Services secured a 70% ownership stake, with Sakima retaining 30%. Additionally, Dott Services is committed to establishing a mineral processing facility alongside its infrastructure projects.<ref name=":622" /> The company is widely believed to have close ties to Uganda's political elite.<ref name=":622" />

Rwanda has also sought to assert its influence in the region's mining sector. In June 2021, Kagame and Tshisekedi signed an agreement allowing Dither Ltd, a company reportedly linked to the Rwandan military, to refine gold mined by Sakima.<ref name=":622" /> The stated objective of the agreement was to curb revenue streams for armed groups operating in the mining sector. However, the DRC suspended the deal in June 2022, citing Rwanda's alleged support for the resurgent M23 rebellion.<ref name=":622" /> Ugandan officials have argued that Rwanda's increasing support for M23 was motivated, at least in part, by the disruption of its economic ventures in the DRC. During the M23 offensive in Bunagana on 23 March 2022, Ugandan soldiers intervened to safeguard Dott Services personnel and assets. The incident heightened tensions between Uganda and Rwanda, which accused the other of leveraging M23 for economic gain.<ref name=":622" /> In [[Kampala]], officials alleged that Rwandan-backed M23 elements orchestrated the attack to disrupt Uganda's operations in the DRC. Conversely, in Kigali, officials accused Uganda of using M23 affiliates to seize the border town, a crucial transit hub for Dott Services.<ref name=":622" />

=== Motives of M23 ===
Experts argue that M23's motives have neither been consistent over time nor between its members.<ref name=":77">{{Cite report |url=https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5c190ffde5274a466da21704/DRC_case_study.pdf |title=Elite Bargains and Political Deals Project: Democratic Republic of Congo (M23) Case Study |last=Shepherd |first=Ben |date=February 2018 |publisher=[[Office for Conflict, Stabilisation and Mediation|Stabilisation Unit]] |access-date=10 December 2025 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20251210171016/https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/5c190ffde5274a466da21704/DRC_case_study.pdf |archive-date=10 December 2025 }}</ref>{{Reference page|page=10}}<ref name=":78">{{Cite web |last1=Verweijen |first1=Judith |last2=Vogel |first2=Christoph |date=11 February 2025 |title=Why conflict mineral narratives don't explain the M23 rebellion in DR Congo |url=https://www.thenewhumanitarian.org/opinion/2025/02/11/why-conflict-mineral-narratives-dont-explain-m23-rebellion-dr-congo |access-date=10 December 2025 |website=[[The New Humanitarian]]}}</ref> Some identified motives include, but are not limited to, genuine worries for aggression against Congolese Tutsi,<ref name=":77" />{{Reference page|page=10}} territorial expansion and land rights,<ref name=":78" /><ref name=":79">{{Cite report |url=https://ipisresearch.be/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/20240403_Le-M23-version-2-Enjeux-motivations-perceptions-et-impacts-locaux.pdf |title=Le M23 " version 2 ": Enjeux, motivations, perceptionset impacts locaux |trans-title=The M23 "version 2": Local stakes, motivations, perceptions, and impacts |date=4 April 2024 |publisher=International Peace Information Service |language=fr |id=D/2024/4320/03 |access-date=10 December 2025 }}</ref>{{Reference page|pages=19-21}} profit incentives primarily from mineral extraction,<ref name=":78" /><ref>{{Cite report |url=https://jamestown.org/m23-leverages-taxes-and-minerals-to-sustain-drc-operations/ |title=M23 Leverages Taxes and Minerals to Sustain DRC Operations |last=Karamuka |first=Brian G. |date=4 December 2025 |publisher=[[Jamestown Foundation]] Terrorism Monitor |issue=10 |volume=23 |access-date=10 December 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Kamurai |first1=Clarkson |last2=Handler |first2=Brad |last3=Bazilian |first3=Morgan |date=23 April 2025 |title=Illicit mineral supply chains fuel the DRC's M23 insurgency |url=https://www.atlanticcouncil.org/blogs/energysource/illicit-mineral-supply-chains-fuel-the-drcs-m23-insurgency/ |access-date=10 December 2025 |website=[[Atlantic Council]]}}</ref> evasion of arrest,<ref name=":78" /> and desire for power.<ref name=":77" />{{Reference page|page=10}}<ref>{{Cite web |last=Rybalchenko |first=Artem |date=23 May 2025 |title=The Modern-day "Heart of Darkness": How the M23 Movement Threatens stability in Central Africa |url=https://www.theperspective.se/2025/05/23/article/the-modern-day-heart-of-darkness-how-the-m23-movement-threatens-stability-in-central-africa/#:~:text=The%20case%20of%20the%20DRC,wherever%20a%20power%20vacuum%20arises. |access-date=10 December 2025 |website=The Perspective}}</ref> Since its 2022 resurgence, the M23 have stated their concern for Tutsi populations and dismay regarding corruption within the Congolese government.<ref name=":79" />{{Reference page|page=|pages=14-15, 17}}<ref name=":83">{{Cite web |last=Sabbe |first=Brian |date=6 February 2023 |title=Why M23 is not your average rebel group |url=https://ipisresearch.be/weekly-briefing/why-m23-is-not-your-average-rebel-group/ |access-date=10 December 2025 |website=International Peace Information Service}}</ref> Some expert communities argue M23 invoked these worries to both obscure from their other motivations and distract from war crimes which contradict their stated aims;<ref name=":83" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Baudon |first=Antoine |date=9 August 2024 |title=M23: A Forgotten War and an Overshadowed Ceasefire |url=https://icct.nl/publication/m23-forgotten-war-and-overshadowed-ceasefire |access-date=10 December 2025 |website=[[International Centre for Counter-Terrorism]]}}</ref> other communities argue that these motivations, rooted in colonial history,<ref name=":79" />{{Reference page|page=15}} remained true for many fighters and, therefore, can not be discounted.<ref name=":79" />{{Reference page|page=17}}<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Verweijen |first1=Judith |last2=Vogel |first2=Christopher |date=30 May 2023 |title=Why Congo's M23 Crisis Lingers On |url=https://theglobalobservatory.org/2023/05/why-congos-m23-crisis-lingers-on/#:~:text=It%20is%20crucial%20that%20all,a%20key%20driver%20of%20smuggling. |access-date=10 December 2025 |website=[[International Peace Institute]] Global Observatory}}</ref>

Stearns argued that the new M23 offensive was possibly aimed at enforcing the group's inclusion in a [[disarmament, demobilisation, and reintegration]] (DDR) programme. Previous attempts of M23 at becoming part of this process, including after the 2013 agreement, failed due to considerable opposition by the Congolese public. One of the issues hampering any attempts to achieve M23's complete demobilisation is the fact that several members of the rebel group are known to have committed various war crimes over several years of involvement in insurgencies, even before M23 itself had emerged. This makes their integration into the Congolese security forces or rewarding them with amnesties difficult to justify in the DR Congo.<ref name="Sengenya Huon" />

===War crimes ===
On 6 August 2025, the OHCHR reported that between 9 and 21 July, M23 forces, aided by the RDF, killed at least 319 civilians in North Kivu, including 48 women and 19 children, most of whom were farmers camping in their fields.<ref name=":80">{{Cite web |last=Mishra |first=Vibhu |date=6 August 2025 |title=Armed militia kill hundreds in eastern DR Congo |url=https://news.un.org/en/story/2025/08/1165586 |access-date=8 August 2025 |website=News.un.org |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":81">{{Cite web |date=6 August 2025 |title=DRC: Türk appalled by attacks against civilians by Rwandan-backed M23 and other armed groups |url=https://www.ohchr.org/en/press-releases/2025/08/drc-turk-appalled-attacks-against-civilians-rwandan-backed-m23-and-other |access-date=8 August 2025 |website=Ohchr.org/en |publisher=[[Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=7 August 2025 |title=UN accuses M23 of killing 319 civilians in July in eastern DRC |url=https://ici.radio-canada.ca/nouvelle/2184024/onu-rebelles-m23-civils-rdc-republique-democratique-congo |access-date=7 August 2025 |work=Ici.radio-canada.ca |publisher=[[Canadian Broadcasting Corporation]]}}</ref>

